## 1. The standard and the review tool

- **Standard:** National Health Education Standards, 2024, SHAPE America (it calls this the third edition).
  Always named "SHAPE America, 2024". Loaded in data/standards/nhes-2024.txt: 172 indicators, each id with our own words (below),
  `NHES.S<standard>.<span end>.<n>`, spans ending at grade 2, 5, 8 and 12. Eight standards: use health
  information; analyze influences; find valid and reliable resources; communicate with others; decide; set goals;
  practice healthy behaviors; advocate.
- **Review tool:** CDC's Health Education Curriculum Analysis Tool (HECAT), 2021 edition. Topics and healthy
  behavior outcomes are in data/standards/hecat-topics.txt (9 topics with 81 outcomes, plus the combined module).
  The tool is keyed to the 2007 standards; our mapping is docs/standards-notes/health-2007-to-2024.md. Both
  editions are named wherever the tool is used.
- scripts/standards.py checks the standard (1 to 8), the span end (2, 5, 8 or 12) and a one or two digit number; it
  does not check that the number exists, and it does not check HECAT ids. scripts/progression.py checks both
  against the files. The coverage map cells below are not machine-checked.
- **Our words.** Each NHES id in nhes-2024.txt carries one line in Curio Class's own words, and each HECAT outcome
  in hecat-topics.txt one plain line (2026-11-19). Plans cite the id and state its action in these words.
- **Module skill expectations** are keyed to the 2007 indicators. They are read against the 2024 standard of the
  same number; the planner picks the 2024 indicators by matching the skill's verb and records the match on the unit
  plan. Standard 1 is reread against NHES.S1 itself (the 2007 to 2024 note). No indicator-by-indicator crosswalk.

## 2. Reuse terms (read at source, 2026-11-16)

- **SHAPE America, 2024:** the free standards page shows the standards and indicators in full, with no reuse terms
  and a site footer of "All Rights Reserved". The book is sold. So: ids and our own words only, as for every
  all-rights-reserved instrument. No indicator sentence is copied into a page.
- **HECAT 2021:** a CDC publication, a US government work. Its topic names and outcome numbers are used freely.
  We still write outcomes in our own words, so a page never reads as the tool's checklist. Module text is not
  copied into lessons, and no page says Curio Class was scored by or meets the tool.

## 3. What the topic module showed (read in part)

From the Sexual Health module and appendices 3 and 5 (our words):

- Each module lists knowledge expectations by span, each tagged to the outcomes it serves, then skill
  expectations under standards 2 to 8. A curriculum is scored on how much of each list it covers. Standards 2 to 8
  get two scores: whether the skill is named, and whether students practice it.
- Users are told to add, drop or revise expectations to fit local needs. That is the tool's own room for a
  school to leave out a unit.
- Expectations deepen by span. In this module the early span has a handful (body part names, families, safe and
  unsafe touch, germs); the next has about twenty (puberty, relationships, consent, gender identity defined,
  HIV basics); the middle span has many more.
- Appendix 5: a scope and sequence shows balance across topics, reinforces without repeating, builds each skill
  across topics, and is checked with a coverage map of standards by span and topic. A single topic need not
  carry all eight standards; the whole program must.
- Still unread: the other eight modules' span tables. Each band's unit planner reads the modules its units use,
  before the units are planned.

## 4. The band coverage map (draft, ours)

Outcomes are HECAT 2021 numbers (`HECAT.<topic>.HBO<n>` in hecat-topics.txt). "All" means every outcome of the
topic. "(s)" marks a sensitive unit (section 5). This is our placement, made from the outcome list and the module
read; it is checked against each module's span tables when the band's units are planned.

| Topic | K to 2 | 3 to 5 | 6 to 8 | 9 to 12 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Personal health and wellness (PHW) | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 11 | All | All | 1, 2, 5 to 7, 9 to 12 (3, 4, 8 met in 6 to 8) |
| Food and nutrition (FN) | 1, 2, 3, 6, 7 | 1 to 8, 10, 12 | 1 to 9, 11, 12 | 1, 8, 10, 11 (2 to 7, 9, 12 met in 6 to 8) |
| Mental and emotional health (MEH) | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 | 1 to 5, 7 to 9 | All; 6 in a sensitive unit (s) | All; 6 in a sensitive unit (s) |
| Physical activity (PA) | 1, 4, 6 (with PE) | 1 to 7 (with PE) | 1, 2, 5, 7; 3, 4, 6 taught in PE | 1, 2, 5 to 7 (with PE; 3, 4 met in 6 to 8) |
| Safety (S) | 1, 3 to 8 | 1, 3 to 8 | All | 1 to 6, 8 (7 met in 6 to 8) |
| Violence prevention (V) | 1, 3, 7; 8 in body safety (s) | 1, 2, 3, 5, 7; 8, 9 in body safety (s) | 1 to 3, 5 to 7; 4, 8, 9, 10 in a sensitive unit (s) | All; 4, 8, 9, 10 in a sensitive unit (s) |
| Tobacco (T) | 1, 2 | 1, 2, 3, 4 | All | 1, 3 to 5 (2 met in 6 to 8) |
| Alcohol and other drugs (AOD) | 1 (medicine safety) | 1, 2, 3 | 1 to 4, 6, 8 | All |
| Sexual health (SH) | 1, 2, 3 (s) | 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 (s) | 1 to 10 (s) | All (s) |

Key: a number listed in a sensitive part of a cell is taught only in the sensitive unit. MEH 6 is not taught
before grade 6; a grade 3 to 5 mental health unit teaches getting help for sad or worried feelings under the
help-seeking outcome, which the planner confirms from our outcome wording. Care-seeking for checkups (PHW 11) starts
in K to 2 because NHES.S1.2.7 does; using care to treat illness (PHW 10) starts in grade 5. PHW 12 (fads) is first
met in grade 5 (claims that hurt) and judged in grade 7 (progression read, 2026-11-19). The 9 to 12 column lists what the semester carries; an outcome named "met in 6 to 8" is not
taught again there, and any other outcome 6 to 8 already teaches is a revisit (a short review inside another unit),
not taught again in full (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-health-pe-2). Physical activity 3, 4 and 6 in grades 6
to 8 are taught by moving in PE; health names the PE rows instead of planning the same lessons (same card).

Standards by band: every band teaches all eight NHES standards across its topics, and every skill standard
(2 to 8) is practiced in at least two topics in the band. Each band's scope and sequence carries the full map in
this shape, with unit names in the cells, plus a second table of NHES standards by unit.

**Years within a band.** NHES indicators are written per span, not per grade. Bands K to 2, 3 to 5 and 6 to 8
are three grade years each: the scope and sequence names each grade's units, and each topic is claimed at least
in alternate years. Each year touches all eight standards. Band 9 to 12 is one course of one semester, in grade 9: it teaches
in full the outcomes not met by grade 8 and revisits the rest. Its pace, about twice that of earlier grades, is
accepted (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-health-pe-2): the frame runs the goal rows as one weekly thread and names
short units of two to four days. If the semester still cannot hold its outcomes, review gives first: outcomes met
in 6 to 8 become short reviews inside other units, so every new 9 to 12 outcome keeps its time; only if that still
does not fit does a list go to Cory (Desk q-prework-health-pe-1). Topics return at more depth, at least in alternate
years as above; a topic is never a one-off lesson (bar criterion 5).

**In the progression** (data/progressions/health.csv): each indicator has exactly one owner grade, the grade where
it is first fully met, in any grade of its span, and is met no later than the span's last grade; other grades of
the span that prepare or reuse it carry `~CODE`. For 9 to 12 the owner grade is the grade of the semester course
(grade 9 unless the frame says otherwise). scripts/progression.py checks this.

## 5. Sensitive topics: all carried, each unit removable

Decided 2026-11-11: Curio Class carries all of the tool's sensitive topics, and each sensitive unit can be left out
without breaking the course. The rule:

1. **Sensitive content lives in its own units.** These units are sensitive: sexual health (every band); body
   safety, meaning unwanted touch and abuse (V 8 from K, V 9 from grade 3; K to 5); sexual violence, abuse and dating violence (V 4, 8, 9;
   6 to 12); suicide and self-harm (MEH 6, V 10; 6 to 12). No sensitive content sits inside a non-sensitive unit.
2. **No skill lives only in a sensitive unit.** Each NHES indicator claimed in a sensitive unit is also claimed
   in a non-sensitive unit of the same band. A sensitive unit practices a skill already taught elsewhere.
3. **Getting help is taught twice.** How to tell a trusted adult and where to find help is taught in a
   non-sensitive unit (safety or mental health) in every band, so leaving out a sensitive unit never leaves a
   student without it.
4. **No later unit assumes a sensitive unit,** except a later sensitive unit on the same topic.
5. **Each sensitive unit has a leave-out note** on its first page: what it covers, the outcomes and indicators
   that go with it, what the course still meets without it, who is affected by leaving it out (for growing-up
   content, children whose bodies change early), and that the school or family decides. Correct body part names
   are first taught in non-sensitive terms in Personal health and wellness, K to 2, so leaving out a sexual health
   unit never removes them; this is the one place the sensitive-unit rule is eased, and the easing covers one plain
   sentence of names and the idea of private, nothing more. The row names penis, vulva, breasts and bottom (Cory,
   2026-11-21). The family note for that row lists the words first, says there are no pictures, offers preview, and
   lets a family quietly keep a child out of that one part of the day. A person reads the row and its family note
   before either is published. For a sensitive unit, a family note
   (English and Spanish) goes home before it, with the outline and how to opt out, in the school's own process.
6. **Day counts.** A sensitive unit's days are counted as optional. A school that leaves it out has those days free;
   the scope and sequence names a non-sensitive unit to extend.
7. **Content.** Sensitive units are medically accurate, age-matched to the tool's span expectations, inclusive,
   free of scare tactics and shame, and dated.
8. **Sensitivity read.** Each sensitive unit is also read by the sensitivity agent before it goes live
   (docs/FOUNDATION.md section 7; docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md), beside the read in section 6.

## 6. The expert read (bar criterion 7)

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe): "no outside readers available. use a self built
expert agent". No paid read and no spend. Criterion 7 is met this way:

- **Who reads.** A health expert agent, built for this subject from the expert read section of
  docs/agents/lenses/health.md. It is the named reader; its name in the record is "Curio Class health expert agent".
- **What it reads.** Every health unit plan and every health day, before the unit's Exit check, for medical and
  scientific accuracy: facts, figures with their source and year, correct terms for the age, and safe messaging on
  sensitive topics. It reads against current public health sources, not memory alone.
- **The record.** Each read is dated and kept with the unit (the reader, the date, what was checked, what was
  fixed). A unit or day changed after its read is read again. A figure older than its source's latest edition is
  a finding.
- **Public copy.** The record stays in our docs. A course page may say: "Figures last checked against their
  sources on DATE. A school's health staff should read it too." It never says or suggests a clinician read it, and
  never names how the read was done (public pages do not describe our tools).
- **The job brief** for this reader (sources by topic, what to record, web access needed) is written when the
  first health unit plan is ready. It never claims the
  content was reviewed or approved by anyone (AGENTS.md).

## 7. Left out, and why

- No separate pre-kindergarten indicators exist: the PreK-2 span is taught from kindergarten, so all 172 are in
  play.
- The National Consensus for School Health Education set of the same name (2022): a different publisher; one set is
  loaded.
- The combined module (CHE) as a topic: it has no outcomes of its own.
- State health standards and state-required topics (for example, specific drug or safety laws): the course page
  says a school adds them.

## 8. Leans on the core progressions and other subjects

- **Physical education:** the physical activity topic is shared. Health teaches why and planning (PA outcomes
  as knowledge and goal-setting); PE teaches the moving. Units in each name the other.
- **Science:** body systems lean on the life science progression (science:MS-LS1-1 in grade 6, MS-LS1-3 in grade
  7); a health unit uses that model and does not reteach it. Germs, vaccines, puberty and body part names are needed
  before or outside the science course, so health states those plain facts itself, with their source.
- **Each lean's core code** is in the cross_subject column of data/progressions/health.csv.
- **Math:** reading food labels, serving sizes and simple data on behavior lean on ratio and statistics (grades
  6 to 7); health uses the skill, math teaches it.
- **ELA:** analyzing influences (standard 2) and finding valid sources (standard 3) lean on the reading information
  and research standards; advocacy (standard 8) leans on argument writing and speaking.
- **SEL:** communication, decision and goal skills overlap with the SEL courses; the SEL course page and this one
  name each other so a skill is not taught twice in the same year.

## 9. Existing topic stubs

The 21 health stubs in data/modules.csv are stand-ins from before the plan. Each band's scope and sequence keeps, renames or drops
each with its reason: body-systems content is used from science, not retaught; digital wellness sits inside MEH, S
and V as skills; first aid sits in Safety.
# Health: approach

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-1). Written 2026-11-16, fixed 2026-11-19 after a cold read
(docs/reviews/prework-health-read-2026-11-19.md); fields placed by number 2026-11-20
(docs/reviews/prework-health-fixes-2026-11-20.md).** This is the layer 3 approach page for health. It fills the
unit and section plans' subject slots (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) under the shared page docs/APPROACH.md. The
standard, coverage map and sensitive-unit rule are in docs/courses/health/STANDARD.md; the bar is the health part of
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md; the lens is docs/agents/lenses/health.md. Fields not named on this page (unit fields 1,
3, 4 and 6; section fields 2, 4, 10 and 11) are filled as UNIT-PLAN.md says, with nothing added for health.

## What a health unit carries (unit field 2)

- **The standards**: each NHES id with its action in our own words (STANDARD.md sections 1 and 2; the indicator's
  wording is never quoted).
- **The behavior outcomes** it is for (HECAT 2021 numbers; our wording is in data/standards/hecat-topics.txt).
  Every day ties to one (bar criterion 1).
- **Three objectives**: one knowing, one valuing (what the student thinks matters, in a character's case or as a
  belief about what most peers do) and one doing (criterion 8).
- **The skill** it teaches or practices: one of NHES standards 2 to 8 (influences, resources, communication,
  decisions, goals, practicing behaviors, advocacy), with its steps written out.
- **The facts that matter for the decision**: short, accurate and dated (criterion 2). A unit lists its figures
  with their source and year, so they can be checked when they go stale.
- **The situations**: three to five realistic scenarios at the students' age, used for practice and the check.

## Kinds of day (principle 2)

- **Understanding.** The facts a decision needs, what peers really do (a unit uses a current national survey figure
  for its age group, with its source and year; below grade 6, where no national use survey of the age exists, the
  unit states the norm in plain words and may give the nearest figure, named for its age group (that national youth use
  surveys begin at grade 6 is from memory, checked at the STANDARD.md section 6 read); that students tend to
  overestimate risk behavior is from memory and not yet read), and the influences on a choice (criterion 4). Ends
  in a decision or a belief, not a list of facts.
- **Fluency.** A skill taught in the five steps the bar names: why it matters, the steps, a model, practice on
  real situations, feedback (criterion 3). Short scripted rehearsals in pairs: a refusal, an "I" statement,
  asking an adult for help.
- **Application.** The skill used on a new scenario, or on the student's own life: a goal plan, a decision
  worked through, an advocacy piece for a real audience, a talk with a family member (criterion 6).
- **Check.** A performance task: the student uses the skill on a scenario not practiced in class, or shows a
  finished plan or advocacy piece, scored with a guide on the skill steps. Never a fact-recall test as the
  end-of-unit assessment.
- **Respond.** Acts on the check: re-rehearse the step marked "not yet" with a new scenario.

## The end-of-unit assessment (unit field 5)

The unit check is teacher-judged on the skill steps, with one short facts-in-use item per fact the unit lists.
**Valuing** is checked with a scenario that asks what a good choice would be for this person and why, scored on
whether the reason uses a unit fact, never on which choice.

## Marking and response (unit field 7)

The unit check is marked by the teacher on the skill steps, with no hands-up self-marking. The respond-day groups
come from the class record sheet.

## Tools and supplies (unit field 8; instruments in section field 5)

A tool may give practice with feedback on facts that support a decision, on reading a label or a source, and on
sorting scenarios; it may give a private self-check. It may not collect any student's health information, ask
about a student's own behavior, stand in for the rehearsal with people, or give medical advice.

## Family note (unit field 9)

Every unit gives a home task (a talk with a trusted adult the student chooses, or a short list to read together); a
student with no adult to talk with can use a school adult. Family notes are in English and Spanish, written when the
unit is finished; a unit with a leave-out note gets the longer form (what it covers, the dates, how to opt out: in a
school through the school, at home the parent decides).

## Sources (unit field 10)

A unit's field 10 names the source and year of every figure it lists, and points to its dated read record
(STANDARD.md section 6).

## Standards and the teacher page (section field 1)

Each section names the NHES standard and indicator ids it works on, and the behavior outcome. A section that
gives facts names the decision the facts are for. What the teacher page must explain:

- **Which lines of "What a health day never does"** (below) matter most in the unit.
- **The teacher's own views.** Every unit's teacher page has a short note on the assumptions the topic may touch
  (family structure, food, bodies, faith) and one way to keep them out of the room.
- **If a student tells you something.** Every sensitive unit's teacher page says: listen, do not promise to keep
  it secret, follow the school's duty to report, and tell the student who the next adult is. For a parent teaching
  at home it names the national child abuse hotline and the 988 crisis line by purpose, and says a child's
  disclosure is not handled alone.

## What a health day never does

- No scare tactics, shock images or worst cases offered as the usual outcome.
- No blame of a person, a body or a family type for an illness.
- No weighing, no calorie counting by students, no "good" or "bad" foods: foods that help, and foods to have less
  often.
- No method detail on suicide or self-harm, and no lesson on them without a help line and an adult plan.
- No medical advice, and no claim that a product or practice treats anything.
- No task asks a student about their own drug use, sexual activity, diet, weight, mental health, abuse or family.

## The daily evidence (section field 3)

- **A fluency or application day** ends with the skill used once more, alone, on a short scenario: a written
  line of what the student would say or do, using the steps. K to 2 students say or act it to the teacher or a
  partner, and the teacher marks a short list of named students.
- **An understanding day** ends with one question that asks for a choice and a reason that uses a fact from the
  day.
- **Twice a unit**, a self-check: the student rates their own use of the skill steps and names one next step
  (criterion 8).

The teacher sorts into met, partly and not yet, with no mark. No evidence asks a student to disclose their own
behavior, health or family. Scenarios are about characters, and personal plans stay with the student unless
they choose to share.

## What is carried (section field 5)

What is carried in a health unit is the skill's step card: the steps of the unit's one skill, in short words. The
teacher holds the model on the first fluency day (says each step aloud on a scenario). On the second fluency day
pairs hold the card and the teacher only prompts. The card is withdrawn on the first application day, and the
section plan names that day. The unit check is always taken without the card. A skill returns in a later unit with
the card for one day, then without it. K to 2 use the same steps as three or four pictured words said aloud.

## How a day opens (section field 6)

A day opens with a short scenario or a question about what most students that age do, five minutes at most. In K
to 5, anything new is taught before it is tried; the fluency day always teaches the steps before pairs try them.

## Rehearsal in sensitive units (section field 7)

In sensitive units rehearsal uses written scripts of the refusal line only. The teacher or a card plays the
pressure; no student plays a person who pressures, abuses or threatens.

## Practice that returns (section field 8)

Each skill returns in later units on other topics: refusal taught in tobacco returns in alcohol and in safety.
Each topic returns in the next year at more depth. Getting help from a trusted adult returns every year.

## Supports (section field 9)

- Scenarios offer names, families and settings that vary, and the teacher can swap them for local ones
  (criterion 9).
- Every unit with personal content gives a way to take part without sharing.
- Every unit names where students and families can find help, in plain words.
- Sensitive units follow STANDARD.md section 5: their own units, a leave-out note, a family note before them.
- **Personal goals** come from a short, safe list (sleep, water, handwashing, time outside, screen breaks, a kind
  act, asking for help), never about weight, body shape, food restriction or calories; a character's goal is always
  an option. From grade 7 a student may instead choose their own healthy behavior as a goal, under the same limit
  (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-health-2).
- **English learners** (C11): each unit teaches six to ten health words in depth, with correct body part names
  from the start, and gives a sentence frame for each skill step (for example "I won't do that. I'm going to ...").
  A key line says: the right idea in another language or by pointing is a language gap; a wrong idea is a content
  gap.
- **Access and check accommodations**: any rehearsal, exit or check item can be done in writing, by pointing at a
  card, by a tool or with an adult; no student must speak aloud. **Reading load**: students read at most a short
  scenario alone; the rest is read aloud or pictured. **Extension**: the same skill on a harder or less familiar
  scenario, or a skill returned in another topic, never the next grade's standard (C10).

## Pacing

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe): high school health is one semester. Default time,
ours and untested: K to 5 about 30 sessions a year of 30 minutes; 6 to 8 about 45 sessions a year of 45 minutes;
9 to 12 about 80 days of 45 minutes. Each band frame may change these with a reason, and each band's scope and
sequence names its short path (bar C2). How a band splits into grades is in STANDARD.md section 4. The national
figure: the National Health Education Standards (second edition, 2007, page 76, Joint Committee on National Health
Education Standards) recommend at least 40 hours a year in pre-K to grade 2 and 80 hours a year in grades 3 to 12.
Our defaults come to about 15 hours a year in K to 5, 34 in 6 to 8 and 60 in the high school semester, so they are
below it. Cory kept ours (2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-2): health shares the day with every other subject, so the
course page names the national figure and the gap so a school can add time.

## Evidence and sources (C13, C16)

Curio Class names the behaviors a unit is for. It does not claim students will change them, and it has not been
tried with children. Each course has a one-page logic model. What this page rests on: HECAT 2021 chapters 4 and 5
(read in full by the expert reader), its Appendix 3 and 5 and the Sexual Health module in part (STANDARD.md section
3), the NHES 2024 indicators (our wording in data/standards/nhes-2024.txt); everything else is from memory and
marked so.
# Lens: health

Pre-work, 2026-11-16. The expert reads this before any health course frame, scope and sequence, unit plan or day.
It is a way of seeing, not a bar: the bar is the health part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md. The standard is
SHAPE America, 2024 (data/standards/nhes-2024.txt), reviewed with CDC's HECAT 2021, which is keyed to the 2007
standards (docs/standards-notes/health-2007-to-2024.md). The coverage map and sensitive-unit rule are in
docs/courses/health/STANDARD.md. The approach page is docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md (approved 2026-11-21).

## What good teaching of health looks like

**It aims at behavior.** Every unit is for named healthy behaviors, and every day ties to one. Knowing about
health is the means. A unit that ends with students who know more and do nothing different has missed.

**Facts are few, accurate and useful.** Students learn the facts a decision needs, not everything known. Each
figure is current and dated. Medical words are correct for the age (the tool expects correct body part names from
the start).

**Skills are taught like any skill.** A skill (refusing, asking for help, deciding, setting a goal, analyzing an
influence) is explained, broken into steps, modeled, practiced on real-feeling situations and given feedback.
Then it comes back in other topics. A skill named once on a poster is not taught.

**Norms and pressure are worked on directly.** Students often think more of their peers take risks than really do.
Good lessons show the real figures, look at where pressure comes from (friends, media, ads, family) and practice
answering it.

**No fear, no shame.** Scare tactics and shock images do not change behavior and can harm. Lessons are warm,
direct and non-judgmental. They never single out a student, a family type or a culture.

**Families and trusted adults are in the loop.** Units send something home, and students practice talking with an
adult. Every unit names where to find help.

**Inclusive and safe to learn in.** Students differ in family, culture, body, identity, means and experience.
Scenarios vary, language is inclusive, and no task asks a student to disclose their own behavior or history.
Teachers get a note on their own bias where it matters.

**Enough time, spread out.** Topics return across years at more depth. A one-time assembly or single lesson on a
topic is not health education.

## Errors reviewers catch

### Scope and sequence

- A band that does not reach all eight standards, or a skill standard practiced in only one topic.
- No coverage map, or a map whose cells name no units.
- A topic taught once in a band with nothing after it.
- A sensitive unit that is not removable: a skill only taught there, a later unit that needs it, or no leave-out
  note (STANDARD.md 5).
- Getting help taught only inside a sensitive unit.
- Indicators claimed in the wrong span.

### Unit

- No named behavior outcome, or days that tie to none.
- A fact-recall test as the end-of-unit assessment.
- A skill named with no steps, no model or no practice.
- Scare tactics, shock images, worst cases as the norm, or moralizing.
- Figures with no source or year, or out of date.
- Content above or below the age (check against the tool's span expectations).
- No link home and no family note where one is needed.
- Wording copied from SHAPE America's indicators (ids and our own words only).

### Day

- A task that asks a student about their own drug use, sexual activity, diet, weight, mental health, abuse or
  family.
- A scenario that blames a person for an illness, a body or a family type.
- A body or weight focus in nutrition or activity (no weighing, no calorie counting by students, no "good" and
  "bad" foods).
- A suicide or self-harm lesson that describes methods, or is taught with no help line and no adult plan.
- A tool that collects health information or gives medical advice.

### Across subjects

- Health reteaching a science model (body systems, germs) instead of using it.
- Health and PE both planning the same physical activity lesson, or neither planning it.

## What this lens does not cover

- Medical and scientific accuracy as a full check: that is the expert read below (criterion 7), a separate pass
  with its own dated record.
- Local law and policy on sensitive topics (consent rules, opt-out law): the school's.
- Counseling and crisis response: the school's staff.

## The expert read (bar criterion 7)

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe): a self-built health expert agent meets criterion 7;
no outside or paid reader. The rule is docs/courses/health/STANDARD.md section 6. The agent doing the read takes
this lens and checks every unit plan and every day:

1. **Facts.** Each health, medical or scientific statement is true as of today, checked against a current public
   health source (a government health agency, a national medical or public health body, a peer-reviewed review),
   not memory alone.
2. **Figures.** Each figure names its source and year, matches that source, and is from its latest edition.
3. **Terms.** Body part names, conditions and medical words are correct and fit the span (the tool's
   expectations).
4. **No harm.** No method details for suicide or self-harm; a help line and an adult plan are present; no diet,
   weight or calorie advice to students; no medical advice; no claim a product or practice treats anything.
5. **Sensitive units.** Medically accurate, inclusive, free of scare tactics and shame (STANDARD.md section 5).
6. **Leans.** Science facts used in health match the science course's model.

Record per unit: reader ("Curio Class health expert agent"), date, sources checked, findings, fixes. Any change to
a unit or day after its read sends it back for a new read.

## Sources, read into our own words

- SHAPE America, National Health Education Standards, 2024: the eight standards and their span indicators (free
  page, all rights reserved).
- CDC, HECAT 2021: Appendix 3 (all 81 behavior outcomes), Appendix 5 (using the tool to build a scope and
  sequence, the coverage map), and the Sexual Health module in part (instructions, PreK-2 and 3-5 knowledge
  expectations, scoring).
- The bar's own reading of HECAT chapters 4 and 5, as recorded on docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md: the
  characteristics of effective health education (behavior focus, skills, norms, no scare tactics, enough time).
- From memory, not opened for this lens: research on fear appeals and on norm misperception in adolescent risk
  behavior, and safe messaging guidance on suicide. Treat these as background, not cited claims.

