| 1 | Wellness and a goal. *How do the parts of a person's wellness work together, and what one goal is worth a semester?* | 4 | g9-phw-strengths-wellness-links; g9-phw-goal-assess-write | NHES.S1.12.1; NHES.S1.12.2; NHES.S6.12.1; NHES.S6.12.2; NHES.S6.12.3 | Setting goals (standard 6): the goal-plan at span-12 depth | Day 1 (hook: a character with a new job, a sport and a family duty whose sleep, mood and friendships move together): how the parts of wellness (physical, mental and emotional, social, and the setting around a person) connect to health results (NHES.S1.12.2); retrieval of the grade 8 goal log and factor levels. Day 2: ways to build strengths that help one person and a group (a household's shared sleep routine, a team's water habit, a friend group that walks), applied in a plan for a character and for a group the student chooses (NHES.S1.12.1). Day 3: assess one's own health and the factors that bear on a goal, on a sheet that stays with the student (NHES.S6.12.1); choose a goal process alone, with support or together, and why it fits (NHES.S6.12.2); write a goal from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior (never weight, shape, food restriction or calories) with how it supports health (NHES.S6.12.3); the goal log starts. Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: how two parts of a character's wellness connect, a strength that helps a group, the character's goal written with its process and health link; the student's own goal sheet is checked for its parts only (or the school-day goal). Out: carrying out and evaluating the goal (the weekly slots and unit 16). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO7. Tools: the goal log (one paper page a week). Curio Class writes: two cases, the goal log, the goal-plan step card at span-12 depth. Cost (class of 30, provisional): $4 printing | First because the goal thread needs a written goal by day 4 so the first weekly slot (week 2) can start, and the wellness links give the goal its reason. Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health, g8-phw-goal-monitor and g8-phw-goal-effect (health grade 8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add teen sleep amounts; one sourced fact line inside day 1 | The self-assessment and the goal stay with the student; never ask about a student's own health, body or family. Goals from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior, never about weight, shape, food restriction or calories (APPROACH.md, Supports); a character's goal is always an option, and a school-day goal stands for the doing record. Goals fit any body and any condition. Teacher's own views note (bodies, food, family). Agent reads: sensitivity (students with chronic conditions and disabilities; a national eating disorders association's guidance for schools) |
| 2 | What gets in the way. *Why does a teen who wants to be more active often not do it?* | 4 | g9-phw-factors-and-behavior | NHES.S1.12.6; NHES.S1.12.7; NHES.S1.12.8 | Health information (standard 1) with the influence check at four levels | Day 1 (norm: the latest CDC Youth Risk Behavior Survey share of high school students active at least 60 minutes every day, source and year at the expert read): how factors at the personal, social, community and policy levels connect and together shape a result (NHES.S1.12.6), on a physical activity case (a character who wants to walk to school or join a team; grade 8 used sleep and school start time, so this case is new). Day 2: what helps and what gets in the way of a behavior, sorted by level (NHES.S1.12.7). Day 3: how confidence in one's own ability and one's sense of risk change a behavior, with the character's two tries (NHES.S1.12.8). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen activity case: map its factors and how they connect, name two barriers and one help, explain how confidence and risk sense would change the behavior. Out: training principles and fitness (PE). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6, HBO7. Tools: the four-level factor map. Curio Class writes: the case in two parts, a factor map sheet. Cost: $3 | Second because the four-level factor map is the tool units 3, 7 and 13 use, and students meet it before their first goal slot (week 2). Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health (health grade 8), the year before. PE grades 9 to 12 teaches the moving (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PA module 9 to 12 read may add muscle and bone activity guidance; one sourced line inside day 1 | No ranking of students' own activity, body size or fitness; a body of any size, ability or condition can be active; a student who uses a wheelchair or cannot run is in every case's range. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities and chronic conditions) |
