docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:216:| 1 | Wellness and a goal. *How do the parts of a person's wellness work together, and what one goal is worth a semester?* | 4 | g9-phw-strengths-wellness-links; g9-phw-goal-assess-write | NHES.S1.12.1; NHES.S1.12.2; NHES.S6.12.1; NHES.S6.12.2; NHES.S6.12.3 | Setting goals (standard 6): the goal-plan at span-12 depth | Day 1 (hook: a character with a new job, a sport and a family duty whose sleep, mood and friendships move together): how the parts of wellness (physical, mental and emotional, social, and the setting around a person) connect to health results (NHES.S1.12.2); retrieval of the grade 8 goal log and factor levels. Day 2: ways to build strengths that help one person and a group (a household's shared sleep routine, a team's water habit, a friend group that walks), applied in a plan for a character and for a group the student chooses (NHES.S1.12.1). Day 3: assess one's own health and the factors that bear on a goal, on a sheet that stays with the student (NHES.S6.12.1); choose a goal process alone, with support or together, and why it fits (NHES.S6.12.2); write a goal from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior (never weight, shape, food restriction or calories) with how it supports health (NHES.S6.12.3); the goal log starts. Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: how two parts of a character's wellness connect, a strength that helps a group, the character's goal written with its process and health link; the student's own goal sheet is checked for its parts only (or the school-day goal). Out: carrying out and evaluating the goal (the weekly slots and unit 16). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO7. Tools: the goal log (one paper page a week). Curio Class writes: two cases, the goal log, the goal-plan step card at span-12 depth. Cost (class of 30, provisional): $4 printing | First because the goal thread needs a written goal by day 4 so the first weekly slot (week 2) can start, and the wellness links give the goal its reason. Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health, g8-phw-goal-monitor and g8-phw-goal-effect (health grade 8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add teen sleep amounts; one sourced fact line inside day 1 | The self-assessment and the goal stay with the student; never ask about a student's own health, body or family. Goals from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior, never about weight, shape, food restriction or calories (APPROACH.md, Supports); a character's goal is always an option, and a school-day goal stands for the doing record. Goals fit any body and any condition. Teacher's own views note (bodies, food, family). Agent reads: sensitivity (students with chronic conditions and disabilities; a national eating disorders association's guidance for schools) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:217:| 2 | What gets in the way. *Why does a teen who wants to be more active often not do it?* | 4 | g9-phw-factors-and-behavior | NHES.S1.12.6; NHES.S1.12.7; NHES.S1.12.8 | Health information (standard 1) with the influence check at four levels | Day 1 (norm: the latest CDC Youth Risk Behavior Survey share of high school students active at least 60 minutes every day, source and year at the expert read): how factors at the personal, social, community and policy levels connect and together shape a result (NHES.S1.12.6), on a physical activity case (a character who wants to walk to school or join a team; grade 8 used sleep and school start time, so this case is new). Day 2: what helps and what gets in the way of a behavior, sorted by level (NHES.S1.12.7). Day 3: how confidence in one's own ability and one's sense of risk change a behavior, with the character's two tries (NHES.S1.12.8). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen activity case: map its factors and how they connect, name two barriers and one help, explain how confidence and risk sense would change the behavior. Out: training principles and fitness (PE). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6, HBO7. Tools: the four-level factor map. Curio Class writes: the case in two parts, a factor map sheet. Cost: $3 | Second because the four-level factor map is the tool units 3, 7 and 13 use, and students meet it before their first goal slot (week 2). Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health (health grade 8), the year before. PE grades 9 to 12 teaches the moving (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PA module 9 to 12 read may add muscle and bone activity guidance; one sourced line inside day 1 | No ranking of students' own activity, body size or fitness; a body of any size, ability or condition can be active; a student who uses a wheelchair or cannot run is in every case's range. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities and chronic conditions) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:218:| 3 | Fair chances. *Why do two neighborhoods a few miles apart not have the same health?* | 3 | g9-phw-conditions-equity | NHES.S2.12.2; NHES.S2.12.3 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check at the community and environment levels | Day 1: two neighborhoods' data cards (invented for practice: parks, grocery stores, clinic hours, bus routes, air quality, average income) and one real agency figure on a health gap by income or place, with source and year (picked at the unit plan from a federal or state health data page, checked at the expert read): how social conditions such as income and neighborhood shape behaviors, results and fairness in health (NHES.S2.12.2). Day 2: appraise how factors from the personal to the environmental affect fairness, and what each level can change (NHES.S2.12.3), with community strengths and action named as well as gaps. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen pair of places: assess a gap and appraise which levels shape it. Rates and percents only as grade 6 and 7 math taught them. Out: blame of a person or a group; any student's own neighborhood. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6. Curio Class writes: data cards, a fairness map. Cost: $4 | After unit 2's four levels, which it applies to places. Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add environmental health expectations; one data card | Families and neighborhoods in the room vary: study places, not people; no student describes their own neighborhood, income or insurance; no deficit framing of a community, and every place is shown with its strengths. Agent reads: sensitivity (students and families with low income, and neighborhoods named in public data) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:219:| 4 | Getting care. *What makes it easy or hard for a teen to reach health care and information they can trust?* | 4 | g9-phw-access-to-care | NHES.S1.12.9; NHES.S3.12.1; NHES.S3.12.2 | Accessing information and services (standard 3): help-steps and source-check | Day 1: how reaching care relates to wellness (a character's untreated earache, a missed checkup, a delayed eye exam) (NHES.S1.12.9). Day 2: how easy it is to reach trusted adults, professionals and resources, mapped on an invented town and on the school's own list (nurse, counselor, a clinic, a pharmacy, help lines named by purpose) (NHES.S3.12.1). Day 3: what opens or blocks access to trustworthy information and services: cost, insurance, transport, hours, language, privacy, and confidential care, whose rules for teens vary by state (the school adds its state's rule; no legal advice) (NHES.S3.12.2). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: map the character's options and blocks and pick a route with a reason; readiness check on g7-phw-living-with-conditions in the last ten minutes. Out: insurance plan details. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO10, HBO11. Curio Class writes: the town map, the school-list template, access cards. Cost: $3 | A getting-help unit in a non-sensitive place, so units 19 and 21 can be left out. After unit 3, because cost and place are the blocks unit 3 studied. Retrieval: g8-phw-health-care-services (health grade 8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add "know your health history and how to share it"; one scene inside day 2 | Access and cost differ; no student is asked about their own insurance, care or health history. Confidential care is a fact about rights whose details vary by state, never advice. Agent reads: sensitivity (students and families without insurance or with low income) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:220:| 5 | Habits that prevent. *Which habits really cut down illness and injury, and how do people live well with an ongoing condition?* | 3 | g9-phw-evaluate-habits | NHES.S1.12.3; NHES.S1.12.4 | Health information (standard 1): evaluating evidence with the goal-plan's look-back | Day 1: evaluate behaviors for how well they prevent or cut down illness and injury (seat belts, helmets, hand washing, vaccines, sunscreen, sleep), each with its agency source and year (NHES.S1.12.3). Day 2: evaluate habits that support health, including care of an ongoing condition (an asthma action plan, diabetes care at school, a seizure plan), with characters who live full lives and name their supports (NHES.S1.12.4); the readiness results from unit 4 shape day 2's opening. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen set of habits: rank and justify how well each prevents harm, and judge a character's condition-care routine. Out: treatment choices and medical advice. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO5, HBO6, HBO9. Curio Class writes: habit cards with sources, three condition-care cases. Cost: $3 | After unit 4, because condition care uses the care map. The readiness check sits on unit 4's last day because g7-phw-living-with-conditions was last used in grade 7 (health grades 6 to 8), two grades back. Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community, the year before. Biology grade 9 runs the same year; health states its own plain facts with sources (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add self-exams and screenings by age; one sourced line inside day 1 | No blame for illness or injury; a student with a condition is never singled out or asked to explain it. Vaccines: plain facts, no debate staged, a family's choices never discussed. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with chronic conditions and disabilities; CDC chronic disease and vaccine pages) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:221:| 6 | Information you can trust. *Should a teen athlete buy a protein powder, and what do I do about a post that says so?* | 4 | g9-fn-information-skill; g9-meh-counter-disinformation | NHES.S1.12.5; NHES.S3.12.3; NHES.S3.12.4; NHES.S3.12.5 | Accessing valid information (standard 3): source-check, with the speak-up plan's counter line | Day 1: how the health information skills of people and organizations shape results (a clear clinic handout against a confusing one, a label read or misread) (NHES.S1.12.5). Day 2: rate resources on accuracy, trustworthiness and availability on one food and supplement question, whether a teen athlete needs a protein powder (NIH and FDA supplement pages and the federal dietary guidelines, latest edition, as the anchor sources; that supplements are not approved for effect before sale is a fact checked at the expert read) (NHES.S3.12.3); choose the accurate sources and use them to answer the question (NHES.S3.12.4). Day 3: misinformation and deliberate disinformation about mental health, drugs, diets or wellness trends, on invented posts: pause, trace, counter kindly, report (NHES.S3.12.5). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen question and source set: rate, choose and answer with the sources used, and write a counter to one invented post. Out: any product name; diet advice. Serves HECAT.FN.HBO1, HBO8; PHW.HBO12; MEH.HBO2, HBO9. Tools: printed source packets (class devices optional). Curio Class writes: source packets, rating sheet, invented posts. Cost: $5 | After unit 4's source-check on care, which this unit deepens. Before unit 7, which judges media among influences. Retrieval: g8-phw-handle-false-messages (health grade 8) and the argument-and-evidence reading of ELA grade 8 unit 2 (RI.8.8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT FN module 9 to 12 read may add energy drink and caffeine expectations; one source card | No product or brand names; posts are invented. No student is asked about their own supplement use, diet or body. No calories, no weight, no "good" or "bad" foods. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with eating disorders or body image concerns; a national eating disorders association's guidance for schools) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:222:| 7 | Influences and my plan. *What pulls on a teen's well-being, and what plan helps manage it?* | 4 | g9-meh-influences-judge-plan | NHES.S2.12.1; NHES.S2.12.4; NHES.S2.12.5 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check turned into a plan | Day 1: judge how influences and behaviors act on each other and on well-being (late-night scrolling and sleep, stress and caffeine, a friend group's habits) on a character's week (NHES.S2.12.1). Day 2: design a plan to handle the influences on health, for the character and, if the student chooses, privately for themselves (NHES.S2.12.4). Day 3: use the resources available to manage an influence (NHES.S2.12.5): each student uses one school resource from unit 4's map on a plan step (the school's help directory, a planner, a quiet space, a study-break routine) and logs that it was used, never why; the teacher records the use. Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: judge the influences and write a plan with one resource. Out: diagnosis of any mental illness. Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO2, HBO4, HBO9. Curio Class writes: the character's week, plan sheet. Cost: $3 | After units 2, 3 and 6, whose levels and source-check feed the influence judgment. Before unit 8, where asking for help is one of the resources. Retrieval: g8-meh-tools-for-influences, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add stress management expectations; one line inside day 2 | Stress is a normal part of life and never equated with illness; no student is asked about their own mental health or feelings; the personal plan stays with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:223:| 8 | Reaching people. *How do I ask for help, or show I care, in a text, an email or face to face?* | 4 | g9-meh-communication-channels; g9-meh-help-and-concern | NHES.S4.12.1; NHES.S4.12.2; NHES.S4.12.3; NHES.S4.12.10 | Communicating (standard 4): the I-statement and listening steps across channels, with the help-steps | Day 1: communicate through many channels and media (talk, text, a post, an email, a clinic portal message): what each is good for, tone, privacy and permanence (NHES.S4.12.1). Day 2: use the skills across relationships: a friend, a manager about a shift that cuts into sleep, a doctor's office (NHES.S4.12.2), drafted on paper templates never sent. Day 3: ask for and give help for health (NHES.S4.12.3), and express understanding and concern for a friend who is stressed, sad or worried: notice, check in, listen, offer, and know when to tell an adult (NHES.S4.12.10); help lines named by purpose, including 988 for anyone in emotional distress by call, text or chat. Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios in two channels and two relationships: a written help request and a concern response; the spoken rehearsal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: suicide and self-harm content (unit 21). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO1, HBO4, HBO8. Curio Class writes: message templates, scenario cards, the help-line card. Cost: $3 | The getting-help unit of the course in a non-sensitive place, so unit 21 can be left out; unit 21 extends it. After unit 7, which named help as a resource. Retrieval: g8-meh-teamwork and g8-meh-express-concern, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add "help a friend reach a counselor"; one scene inside day 3 | Friend scenarios are characters'; no student is asked about their own struggles. If a student tells you something: listen, do not promise to keep it secret, follow the school's duty to report, and tell the student who the next adult is. No real message is sent. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:224:| 9 | Teams and other views. *How does a team get a health job done when its members want different things?* | 4 | g9-meh-teams-negotiate-views | NHES.S4.12.7; NHES.S4.12.8; NHES.S4.12.9 | Communicating (standard 4): listening steps and I-statement in teamwork and negotiation | Day 1: teamwork in many settings (a sports team, a job, a class project, a household) and the roles that get a job done (NHES.S4.12.7); teams of four form and keep together for units 17 and 18. Day 2: negotiation and compromise in different settings: say what each side needs, offer trades, agree on a fair plan (NHES.S4.12.8). Day 3: change how I communicate to suit people with other views and values: a food custom, a family's screen rules, a team's training choice, worded to stay neutral on beliefs (NHES.S4.12.9). Day 4: end-of-unit check: each team plans a short health task while the teacher records each student's teamwork and negotiation moves on a record sheet, plus an unseen written scenario on adapting a message. Out: choosing the advocacy issue (unit 17). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO7, HBO8. Curio Class writes: role cards, negotiation scenes. Cost: $3 | After unit 8's channels, which teams use. Before the mid-course check; the teams formed here run units 17 and 18. Retrieval: g8-meh-teamwork and g8-meh-different-views, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add "respect differences" expectations; one scene inside day 3 | Values scenes stay neutral on faith, culture and family; no student is asked to defend or reveal their own beliefs; teams are set by the teacher so no student is left out. Agent reads: sensitivity (students of every faith and culture) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:225:| 10 | Limits, consent and conflict. *How do I state a limit, give or take back a yes, and settle a conflict before it turns violent?* | 4 | g9-meh-boundaries-consent; g9-v-conflict-manage | NHES.S4.12.4; NHES.S4.12.6 | Communicating (standard 4): boundary steps and conflict steps | Day 1: state limits, give or take back consent and set boundaries in friendships, at work, online and in shared spaces (a shared photo, a borrowed car ride, an extra shift, a group chat): a yes can be changed, and asking before sharing (NHES.S4.12.4); the readiness results on g7-s-online-boundaries shape the opening. Day 2: boundary rehearsal in pairs with scripts. Day 3: prevent, manage and settle conflicts: calm, listen, negotiate, stay out of places where violence is likely, walk away from people who urge it, get help; no violence is an option (NHES.S4.12.6). Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios: the boundary steps and the conflict steps written for characters; the spoken rehearsal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: sexual consent and dating violence (units 19 and 20). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO3, HBO8; V.HBO1, HBO2, HBO5, HBO6. Curio Class writes: boundary and conflict scripts, scenario cards. Cost: $3 | After the mid-course check. The non-sensitive home of the consent and conflict skills, so units 19 and 20 can be left out and extend it. The readiness check on g7-s-online-boundaries (health grade 7, two grades back) sits in the last ten minutes of the mid-course respond day. Retrieval: g8-v-negotiate-compromise, the year before | Candidate: HECAT V module 9 to 12 read may add online harassment expectations; one scene inside day 1 | No sexual content in this unit. Conflict scenes use scripts and stay verbal; no student acts out a fight or plays an aggressor; a card plays any pressure. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:226:| 11 | Nicotine decisions. *When does a vaping choice deserve a careful process, and who can help?* | 4 | g9-t-decision-views-process | NHES.S5.12.1; NHES.S5.12.2; NHES.S5.12.3 | Deciding (standard 5): the decision steps as a careful process, alone, with support or in a group | Day 1 (norm: the latest National Youth Tobacco Survey share of high school students who used e-cigarettes in the past 30 days; most do not): how a nicotine decision looks to different people and communities (a teen, a parent who smokes, a coach, a store owner, a neighborhood with many tobacco ads) (NHES.S5.12.1). Day 2: determine when and why a situation needs a careful decision process (NHES.S5.12.2). Day 3: use the process alone, with support and in a group on a vaping decision, with where to get help to quit: a free national quit line and a federal text program, named by purpose (NHES.S5.12.3). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen nicotine case: say why it needs a careful process and run it. Out: shock images, worst cases. Serves HECAT.T.HBO1, HBO3, HBO5. Curio Class writes: viewpoint cards, decision sheet, invented flavored-product ads with no brand. Cost: $3 | After unit 10's limits, which a refusal inside a decision uses, and before unit 12, which reuses the decision steps on alcohol and drugs. Retrieval: g8-aod-decision-supports-obstacles and g8-t-tobacco-influences, the year before | Candidate: HECAT T module 9 to 12 read may add nicotine pouch expectations; one source card | No blame of a family member who smokes; no student who uses nicotine is singled out, and quit help is offered to the whole class. Pictures only. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who use nicotine or live with someone who does) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:227:| 12 | Alcohol and other drugs. *How do I say no at a party, in a car or at work, and carry out a choice I have made?* | 4 | g9-aod-say-no-across; g9-aod-weigh-options-plan | NHES.S4.12.5; NHES.S5.12.4; NHES.S5.12.6 | Communicating (standard 4): refusal steps across circumstances; deciding (standard 5): weighing options and an action plan | Day 1 (norm: the latest Youth Risk Behavior Survey share of high school students who drank alcohol in the past 30 days; most did not): weigh options against priorities and likely results for an alcohol or drug decision (NHES.S5.12.4); medicine safety (a prescription is for its own person and is never shared; pills not from a pharmacy can contain fentanyl; naloxone can reverse an opioid overdose, and rules on carrying it vary by state), facts checked at the expert read. Day 2: ways to say no across circumstances (a party, a ride, a job, a partner or friend who asks you to ride or use), including never driving after alcohol or other drugs and not riding with someone who has, with a safe-ride plan (NHES.S4.12.5), rehearsed in pairs with scripts. Day 3: create an action plan to carry out a decision, with where to get help to stop use (a free national helpline named by purpose) (NHES.S5.12.6). Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenes: a refusal for two circumstances, options weighed and an action plan for a character; the spoken refusal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: drug-by-drug detail. Serves HECAT.AOD.HBO1 to HBO8; S.HBO2; MEH.HBO5. Curio Class writes: circumstance cards, refusal scripts with a card playing the offer, an action plan sheet. Cost: $3 | After unit 11's careful process, which it reuses. Retrieval: g8-aod-refusal-and-driving and g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT AOD module 9 to 12 read may add cannabis and state law expectations; one sourced line inside day 1 | No student is asked about their own or their family's use; no scare images or worst cases as the norm; a card plays the offer and no student plays the person offering. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who use alcohol or drugs, or live with someone who does; SAMHSA family pages) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:228:| 13 | Safety at many levels. *How do one driver's choice, a family's rules and a state law each change who gets hurt on the road and at work?* | 3 | g9-s-decision-levels-barriers | NHES.S5.12.5; NHES.S5.12.7 | Deciding (standard 5): decision steps with the influence check's levels | Day 1: how a decision could affect health at several levels (a new driver's phone use and passengers: self, riders, other road users, the community) (NHES.S5.12.5), with the national highway traffic safety agency's teen driving pages. Day 2: judge how supports and barriers shape decisions at various levels: graduated licensing rules, a family driving agreement, a first job's safety rules on burns, lifting and hours (federal Department of Labor youth employment pages) (NHES.S5.12.7). Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen case (a home or workplace safety choice): trace its effects by level and judge its supports and barriers; readiness check on g6-fn-eating-patterns in the last ten minutes. Out: driver education. Serves HECAT.S.HBO1, HBO3, HBO4, HBO5, HBO6, HBO8. Curio Class writes: two cases, a level chart. Cost: $3 | After units 11 and 12's decision steps, and before unit 14, whose readiness check it holds. Retrieval: g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT S module 9 to 12 read may add first aid and CPR awareness; one line naming where a school can add training | No crash images; a student who has lost someone on the road or at work is given a way out of the case. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who have lost someone on the road or at work) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:229:| 14 | Meals that work for a group. *How can a household or a team eat well on a small budget?* | 3 | g9-fn-habits-groups | NHES.S7.12.2; NHES.S7.12.4 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) with the goal-plan | Day 1: judge how habits and other factors (time, money, culture, who cooks, school meals) support wellness for people and groups (NHES.S7.12.2); the readiness results on g6-fn-eating-patterns shape the opening. Day 2: show a range of habits that support a group's wellness (NHES.S7.12.4): plan three shared meals on a set budget from an invented store flyer (unit prices as grade 6 math taught them), and each student carries out one habit in class, recorded by the teacher: hand washing and an optional no-cook assembly (a bean and vegetable wrap or a fruit and yogurt cup, under the school's food and allergy rules), or reading a label and portioning a dish to share. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen household case: judge its habits and plan a budget week of shared meals; the day 2 record is carried. Out: weighing, calories, diets. Serves HECAT.FN.HBO1, HBO8, HBO10, HBO11. Curio Class writes: the store flyer (invented for practice), meal plan sheet, no-cook card. Cost: $3 printing; the optional no-cook demonstration about $45 for 30 students; soap and towels from school stock | After unit 13, which holds its readiness check because g6-fn-eating-patterns was last used in grade 6 (health grades 6 to 8), three grades back. Before unit 15, which adapts habits. Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community, the year before | Candidate: HECAT FN module 9 to 12 read may add food safety at home expectations; one line inside day 2 | Food: never ask what a student eats at home; budgets, cultures and school meals vary and are all respected; no good or bad foods, no weighing, no calories. Food demonstration: allergies checked first, no knives (pre-cut items), hand washing, five minutes of clean-up; any student may watch or plan instead of eating. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with low income or too little food at home; students with eating disorders); safety (food handling, allergies) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:230:| 15 | When habits get harder. *What makes a healthy habit easier or harder, and how do I know whether a plan is working?* | 4 | g9-pa-habits-ease-adapt; g9-pa-measure-decisions | NHES.S7.12.1; NHES.S7.12.3; NHES.S5.12.8 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) and deciding (standard 5): the goal-plan and decision steps with simple data | Day 1: assess what makes healthy behaviors easier or harder (time, place, people, money, mood) on a character's activity plan (NHES.S7.12.1). Day 2: measure how well a health decision worked with simple data: a character's one-month activity plan record (invented for practice) and the student's own goal log (planned against done days, a barrier, a change), counted and charted (NHES.S5.12.8); monitoring and revising the activity plan is the goal process in the physical activity topic, practice of NHES.S6.12.5, owned in unit 16. Day 3: change a habit as personal and group needs change (a new job, a new season, an injury): each student revises one step of their goal or school-day goal in the log, with the reason, recorded (NHES.S7.12.3). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen character's data: assess what made it harder, measure how the plan worked, and adapt it. Out: training principles and fitness testing (PE). Serves HECAT.PA.HBO1, HBO2, HBO5, HBO6, HBO7. Curio Class writes: the month record, a chart sheet. Cost: $3 | Late in the goal thread (weeks 11 and 12), so the goal log holds ten weekly entries to measure. Before unit 16, which evaluates the goal. PE grades 9 to 12 teaches training principles and moving (Cross-subject). Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community and g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PA module 9 to 12 read may add "plan activity for life after school"; one line inside day 3 | Data are about the plan, never about the body: no weight, fitness test score or calories; injury and disability are in the cases' range. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities, injuries or eating disorders) |
docs/courses/health-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:231:| 16 | My goal, looked back. *What did ten weeks of my goal do for my health, and what do I keep?* | 2 | g9-phw-goal-carry-revise | NHES.S6.12.4; NHES.S6.12.5; NHES.S6.12.6 | Setting goals (standard 6): the goal-plan's last steps | Carry out a plan that deals with supports and barriers (NHES.S6.12.4) and monitor progress and revise the goal or plan (NHES.S6.12.5): taught and practiced in the ten-minute weekly goal slots, weeks 2 to 11, each slot one step (a step done, a barrier met, a support used, a revision with its reason), written in the goal log. Day 1: gather the log; evaluate how the goal process went and what it did for health and well-being, and what to carry into the next goal (NHES.S6.12.6). Day 2: end-of-unit check: the student's goal log (its process entries; the goal's content may be covered) and a written evaluation, scored on the process steps, never on whether the goal worked or on health; plus an unseen character's log to evaluate. Out: none left; the thread ends here. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO2, HBO7. Curio Class writes: the slot prompts (ten), the evaluation sheet. Cost: $2 | Last of the goal thread, after unit 15 measured and revised the plan; the log is the doing record for NHES.S6.12.4 and NHES.S6.12.5. Retrieval: g8-phw-goal-effect and g8-phw-goal-monitor, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add a long-term health plan expectation; one line inside day 1 | No grade on whether the goal worked; a goal that did not work is information, not failure. A student who kept the goal private submits the school-day goal log. Agent reads: sensitivity |
