13:- **Shared steps with health and PE.** The talk turn card, calm-down steps, I-statement frame, goal steps card and refusal steps use the one set of words in STANDARD section 7 ("Shared steps"), grown for 9 to 12 by the rows: the goal steps card becomes a designed plan (goal, dated steps, the trade-off accepted, a check date, a verdict), stop-think-choose gains a sixth step (look back), the problem size meter becomes a support map, the feelings scale adds what set the feeling off and what it pushes toward, the calm-down steps become a coping plan (five steps, a tool for each setting, a back-up, a review date), the talk turn card adds a check-back step and the I-statement frame adds a request and a check-back ("when..., I feel..., I would like..., does that work for you?"). The refusal second push stays "say it again, move away, tell" (health's boundary steps). Where health or PE teaches a card first in a year, the SEL unit opens with it as retrieval where the school runs that course, and teaches it itself where it does not (Cross-subject, Health and PE).
24:- **Disclosure.** Stress, help, friendship, refusal and upstander units can bring a disclosure. Every unit follows health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Health owns substances, sexual health, dating and dating violence, and suicide and self-harm help (health 9 to 12 units 12, 19, 20 and 21, taken in grade 9); SEL never teaches them, and its teacher notes name the health unit where a case comes near.
25:- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md); no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Before units 5, 9, 18 and 22 (the settled court cases) the agent reads the court and museum sources named under Base text and checks the case is taught with dignity and as settled fact; before units 3, 9, 16 and 22 (culture, identity and difference) it checks that no student is asked to explain their own culture and that each invented partner from another place is shown as one person's way, never a whole culture's; in units 2, 4, 8, 10, 14, 17, 20, 23 and 24 it checks each case stops short of health's topics and names the health unit. Units 2, 6, 12, 18, 21 and 23 also get the safety agent's read. The Spanish family notes get a translation agent's read (For the lead 6). The course-level checks get the answer-key agent's read.
145:| 1 | 9 | A new start. *How do I plan my way into high school, and how will I know the plan works?* | 10 | g9-rel-ask-offer-help; g9-sa-i-can-try; g9-sa-what-interests-me; g9-sm-plan-and-tidy; g9-sm-stay-on-task; g9-soc-read-situation | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-discussion, the same discussion norms the class uses all year | Two groups. Group 1, plan the work: the goal steps card grown into the 9 to 12 plan page (the goal, dated steps, the trade-off accepted, a check date, a verdict); a planning system for an invented first month of high school (seven classes, a team, a long-term project) with what it gives up, followed through a case month and judged (SM5); a focus plan for an invented ninth grader with a project due in three weeks and no one checking daily (work blocks, what to do about the phone, a Friday self check-in), its trade-off, and one part rewritten after the first week (SM3; it also begins AASL.V.D.1, trying again when a task gets hard, placed in SEL by library); a plan for an invented student taking a course that feels too hard (two first steps, a place to ask, what counts as progress, the trade-off of less free time), judged from the first test result (SA7). Group 2, read the place and find help: the six-step stop-think-choose (the grade 8 five steps plus look back) used on three invented settings (a first lab with a new partner, a club fair, a hallway where older students gather), what each choice opens and closes, judged against how the case turned out (SO7); the problem size meter grown into a support map (each size of problem beside the people and offices that fit it, in school and out), built for an invented new student (a missed assignment, a schedule error, a worry that keeps coming back), one ask rehearsed aloud with a partner playing the counselor, and what to do if the first helper cannot help (RS8); privately, a plan to explore two interests the school offers (a course, a club, a job shadow day), its cost in time and what it might give, judged after a set date (SA9). Retrieval only: the grade 8 goal steps, stop-think-choose and problem size meter in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a support map and a focus plan for a new invented student, with the trade-off and a check date) and the help ask rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the Friday self check-in on the student's own planner, kept private. Family activity: the family's own support map of who to call for what. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the six-step stop-think-choose card, the support map. Curio Class writes the cases, the case month and the cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Management.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.5 (unit 20); CASEL.Social Awareness.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (unit 24). | First because every later plan in the band is written on the goal steps plan page and the support map, and a new high school is the first school-day case every student shares. The ELA lean is in the same weeks (Cross-subject). Six sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: a start-of-year readiness read of the grade 8 cards inside the first openings, for a class that did not have SEL 6 to 8 | SA9: the interests list stays with the student, checked only that the steps were followed. RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps (the school's reporting route and the counselor); health grade 9 unit 8 (help and concern, weeks 6 and 7) comes after and opens with the support map. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
146:| 2 | 9 | Stress and coping. *What do I do when the pressure builds, and how do I know what helped?* | 11 | g9-rds-solve-problems; g9-sa-feel-think-link; g9-sa-name-feelings; g9-sa-skills-grow; g9-sm-big-feelings; g9-sm-calm-down | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-w-narrative, a narrative whose character meets pressure and changes | Three groups. Group 1, notice it early: the feelings scale with its two new parts (what set the feeling off, what it pushes toward), used to chart an invented student's school week (a test, a missed bus, good news), find a pattern and plan where an early sign could be caught (SA3); the think-feel-do loop of an invented student who reads a low quiz grade as "I am bad at this", with what the student cares about (doing well for a coach, being seen as capable) shown as part of the loop, and a plan for where to break it (a checked thought, a talk with the teacher) (SA5). Group 2, the coping plan: the calm-down steps grown into a coping plan (the five steps, a tool for each of three settings, a back-up tool, a review date), built for an invented student's heavy week (tests, a game, extra family chores), two tools rehearsed with a partner, judged after the case week (SM2); a response plan for an invented student who snaps at people after hard practices (the trigger, the early sign on the scale, a calm-down step, a planned response, a repair), its cost (leaving a moment early) and a judgment against a second scene (SM1). Group 3, practice and fix: a practice plan on the goal steps card for a skill an invented student finds hard (a language's verb forms, a drum pattern), with what to practice, how often, feedback, rest and a measure, judged from a made-up three-week log, never belief alone (SA8; it also begins AASL.V.D.2, skill grows with practice and help, placed in SEL by library); the class picks one school-day stressor it can act on (a crowded test week, a noisy study hall), sizes it on the support map, designs a fix with its trade-offs, pilots it where the school allows and judges it with the look-back step (RD3). Retrieval only: unit 1's goal plan page and the grade 8 calm-down menu in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen coping plan page for a new invented student and the response plan rehearsed from an unseen scene card. Carry it out: the calm start from the coping plan opens every session from here on. Family activity: a coping plan for one busy family week. Tools: the two-part feelings scale, the coping plan page, the goal steps plan page. Curio Class writes the cases, the week chart and the log. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the pilot's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.1 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.2 (unit 20); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 (unit 23). | Second because every later rehearsal (hard talks, refusals, setbacks) starts from the coping plan, and the first heavy test weeks of high school fall here. Health grade 9 unit 7 (influences and a plan, weeks 5 and 6) teaches its plan for the influences on health in the same weeks; where it comes first, SEL opens with its plan steps. Six sub-skills, three groups. Sessions 11 to 21 of the year, weeks 6 to 11. | Candidate: the class stressor pilot run as the unit's carry-out between sessions | No clinical words; SEL is not therapy and claims to treat nothing. Breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, no spiritual framing. Feelings are named for characters in checks. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health grade 9 unit 7; suicide and self-harm help is health unit 21, weeks 15 and 16). Disclosure: health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the stressor pilot inside school). |
147:| 3 | 9 | Identity and assets. *Who am I in a new school, and what do I bring?* | 8 | g9-rel-different-ways; g9-sa-my-strengths; g9-sa-snap-judgments; g9-sa-tell-truth; g9-sa-who-i-am | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-rl-world-stories, stories in translation whose characters stand between two worlds | Two groups. Group 1, who I am and what I bring: a short identity sketch for an invented ninth grader (or one the student makes up) naming the roles and groups that make up the person, which parts to keep steady in high school and which to try changing, with the trade-off of each, judged after a scene where two groups pull different ways (SA1); an invented character's assets (a skill, a habit, a language spoken at home, a talent from a hobby), where each could help in the new school year, a strength that may be undervalued there, and which match was strongest (SA2); a plan for an invented student acting on honesty where it is awkward (a group project where one member copied work): what to say and to whom, what it costs, judged with the look-back step (SA4). Group 2, check my guesses and work with difference: a plan for an invented ninth grader to test a snap judgment about a new lab partner (what to ask, what evidence would change it), run on the case and judged on whether it caught the error (SA6); working rules for an invented project team from different backgrounds and schools (how to disagree, how fast to reply) that respect each member's habits, what each member adapts, judged after a scene where they are tested (RS3). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 2 (values and integrity) rows and unit 1's stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen plan page (testing a snap judgment and an honest action for a new invented case) and the team-rules rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 1 and 2. Carry it out: one asset the student chooses to use in a class this month, kept private. Family activity: the family's story of one strength someone brought to a new place. Tools: the identity sketch page, the asset plan page, the six-step stop-think-choose card. Curio Class writes the cases and the scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 (unit 19); CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.4 (unit 23). | Third because a student who has a plan and a coping plan can now look at who they are in the new school; the honest-action and snap-judgment plans use unit 1's six steps. The ELA lean is taught just before (Cross-subject). Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 22 to 29 of the year, weeks 11 to 15, then the mid-year check (sessions 30 and 31). | Candidate: a second Fluency case where the honest path costs a friend, for the two-group unit's second rehearsal | SA1, SA2: family and culture are never asked about by name; home languages and culture are welcome when offered; a student may make up the sketch. RS3: no student is asked to explain their own culture; habits come from invented team members. SA6: invented cases only, never blame of a group. SA4: checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
148:| 4 | 9 | Relationships that are good for me. *What makes a friendship healthy, and how do I talk when it is not?* | 11 | g9-rel-friendships; g9-rel-say-no; g9-rel-settle-disagreements; g9-rel-talk-listen; g9-soc-care-when-struggle; g9-soc-read-feelings; g9-soc-thankful | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-discussion, the discussion norms and evidence moves | Three groups. Group 1, talk so it lands: the talk turn card with its new last step (check what landed: the listener says back the main point, then plan the next talk), a hard school-day talk planned on an invented case (telling a lab partner the work split is unfair), rehearsed with a partner and judged from the check-back (RS1); the I-statement frame with its request and check-back ("when..., I feel..., I would like..., does that work for you?"), signs of healthy and unhealthy friendship sorted in an invented case (a friend who copies homework and gets angry when asked to stop), a talk with a limit planned and rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS2); an invented disagreement over a shared school task worked with a partner: each side's interests, two options, one agreed with the frame and a follow-up check, judged after the case's next week (RS5). Group 2, say no and stay steady: a refusal plan made before the pressure arrives (a friend pushing to share test answers in a group chat): the options, an exit, an ally, the value it rests on and the second push ("say it again, move away, tell"), rehearsed against the teacher or a card character (RS6); cues read in an invented school-day scene (a friend quiet at lunch, short replies after class), a check-in planned with two openings, judged once the case reveals more (SO4). Group 3, care and thanks: a support plan for an invented classmate going through a hard few weeks (a lost spot on the team): what to ask first, kinds of care, when to step back and when to bring in an adult, judged against how the friend responds (SO3); a small gratitude habit for an invented student's school week (who, how, when), its trade-off (time, feeling awkward), judged after the case week (SO5). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 5 (communication and conflict) rows, the coping plan and the feelings scale in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen talk plan with the I-statement frame for a new friendship case and the refusal plan rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the check-back step ("so what I hear is...") in every class discussion. Family activity: one family talk with the I-statement frame. Tools: the talk turn card, the I-statement frame, the support map. Curio Class writes the cases and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.1 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.6 (unit 23); CASEL.Relationship Skills.2 (unit 24); CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.3 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.5 (unit 24). | Fourth because hard talks need the coping plan (unit 2) and a sense of what the student values (unit 3). Health grade 9 units 10 (limits, consent and conflict, week 8) and 12 (alcohol and other drugs, weeks 9 and 10) apply these steps to health topics earlier in the fall; where the school runs health in the fall, SEL opens with health's boundary steps and the refusal as retrieval and adds the plan, the ally and the look-back. Seven sub-skills, three groups. Sessions 32 to 42 of the year, weeks 16 to 21. | Candidate: a third Application case set online (a group chat) beside the face-to-face ones | RS2: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health are health's (health units 19 and 20). RS6: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substances are health's (unit 12). RS5: the teacher plays any upset part. SO3: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
149:| 5 | 9 | Norms, media and influence. *Who is shaping my choices, and how do I decide for myself?* | 11 | g9-rds-ask-questions; g9-rds-careful-thinking; g9-rds-check-facts; g9-rds-good-for-us; g9-rds-think-ahead; g9-rds-who-is-affected; g9-soc-fair-rules; g9-soc-why-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate, judging whether reasoning holds and evidence is relevant | Three groups. Group 1, rules and systems: the fairness questions applied to an invented school norm (seniors-only tables in the commons) and to a settled case the courts ruled on, Mendez v. Westminster (1947), where a federal court found the segregation of Mexican American children in California schools unlawful, taught as settled fact with dignity; the unwritten rules of two settings compared (the commons and a club meeting); then a plan to change the school norm through school channels, with its trade-offs (SO6); how an invented video app's feed and an invented school rule each push students' behavior, one change designed to either and judged by who it helps and what it costs (SO8); a week planned for an invented student pulled between late gaming streams, sleep and a team counting on them, judged for balance (RD6). Group 2, open questions and checked facts: the questions, sources and what would change one's mind planned before deciding on an invented school-wide issue (a new phone rule) (RD1); an invented claim spreading at school ("most students sleep six hours") checked against a class data set shown in two plots, a decision on what the data back and what they cannot show (RD2); the short- and long-term results of options in an invented case (posting a clip of a class mishap that would get views), the choice planned with its trade-off and judged as the story unfolds (RD4). Group 3, slow down and widen: the six-step stop-think-choose run on an invented choice pushed by a trending post, compared with the same choice made on a shortcut (RD5); everyone an invented school decision reaches mapped (a club's choice to post photos of an event), the rules it touches named (school photo rules, the people pictured), an option that weighs the far side, judged against a stakeholder the map first missed (RD7). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 1 (how I decide) and unit 4 rows, unit 1's six steps in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen decision page (a new invented claim checked against two plots, the people affected mapped, the choice with its trade-off) and the six-step rehearsal from an unseen card. Carry it out: one question asked before sharing any post this month, kept private. Family activity: the family weighs one household rule with the fairness questions. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the stakeholder map page, two printed plots. Curio Class writes the cases, mock feeds and data set. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.8 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 (unit 23). | Fifth because deciding for oneself under pressure needs the refusal and talk steps of unit 4; the data check leans on Algebra I unit 1 (course days 1 to 17), taught months before. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 43 to 53 of the year, weeks 22 to 27. | Candidate: the same phone-rule decision made twice, once with each group's step, as the Application case | SO6: settled facts the courts ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content; the history itself is social studies'. SO8: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity (the Mendez sources first). |
150:| 6 | 9 | Goals with others. *How do we set a goal together and get it done?* | 9 | g9-rel-lead-follow; g9-rel-stand-up; g9-rel-teamwork; g9-sm-have-a-say; g9-sm-set-goal; g9-sm-step-forward; g9-soc-notice-good; g9-soc-their-view | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-presentation, presenting a plan clearly to an audience | Three groups. Group 1, a shared goal and a start: a term goal designed with a partner on the goal steps plan page (milestones, who does what, the trade-off, a midpoint check), judged and revised after an invented setback (SM4); a start-up plan for something a ninth-grade class lacks (a study group, a welcome table): who to ask, the first step, what could stop it and what it costs, judged after the first invented week (SM6); the class plans a proposal to improve one part of ninth-grade school life and each student designs their part (audience, route, evidence, trade-offs), carried out where the school allows and judged (SM7). Group 2, the team: the team designs its plan for a shared project (roles, norms, how to raise a problem), carries it out and holds a short look-back (RS4; it also begins AASL.III.D.2, the group's learning as one's own job, placed in SEL by library); a plan to lead a team session (an agenda, how the group decides, how to hear every voice), run, then following another member's lead, both judged from team feedback (RS7); credit across a project planned for a team leader (what to notice, when to say it), judged on which credit landed (SO2). Group 3, other views and speaking up: the other side's view in an invented team split written well enough that its holder would agree, a question to check it, judged when the case gives more (SO1); an upstander plan for an invented student left out of a team in a hurtful way (the safest option, the words with the I-statement frame, support afterward, when to report), rehearsed with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act (RS9). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 6 (making a difference) rows and the I-statement frame in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the year-end course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen team plan page and the upstander rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 4 and 5. Carry it out: the shared term goal, checked at the unit's end. Family activity: a shared family goal on the plan page. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the team plan page, the I-statement frame. Curio Class writes the cases and the team task. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the proposal's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.4 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Self-Management.6 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.2 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.9 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Management.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Management.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.1 (unit 24). | Last because a team goal pulls on every earlier unit: the plan page, the coping plan, the talk card and the fairness questions; it ends the year with something the class has built. Health grade 9 units 1 (a goal, week 1) and 9 (teams, week 7) teach the goal process and teamwork earlier; SEL opens with them as retrieval where the school runs health in the fall. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 54 to 62 of the year, weeks 27 to 31, then the year-end check (sessions 63 and 64, week 32). | Candidate: the proposal presented to an invited school adult as the Application audience | SM7: no cause is picked by the course; the class chooses inside school life. RS9: no student plays the unkind part or the one left out; school life only. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the proposal's task, inside school). |
151:| 7 | 10 | Listening to understand. *How do I know I have understood someone, and that they have understood me?* | 10 | g10-rel-talk-listen; g10-sa-feel-think-link; g10-soc-read-feelings; g10-soc-their-view; g10-soc-why-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-sl-seminar, the same student-led discussion norms | Two groups. Group 1, two sides of one talk: a talk planned with a partner on an invented disagreement where each thinks the other is not listening, rehearsed with both people using the check-back step and judged from the partner's account of whether each felt understood (RS1); the strongest form of two invented classmates' opposed views on a school question written out, a conversation planned that finds common ground, judged on whether each person would accept how they were described (SO1); one person's feelings read in an invented scene, then the same moment through the second person's eyes, a response planned for each, judged on which reading the case bears out (SO4). Group 2, two loops and one system: the think-feel-do loops of two invented people in one misunderstanding mapped, with what each cares about shown in the loop, and where either could break it (SA5); how an invented school system (a points reward for attendance) looks to a student it helps and one it pushes out, a change designed and judged from both views (SO8). Retrieval only: grade 9 units 2, 4, 5 and 6 rows (the feelings scale, the talk turn card with its check-back) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen page (two views of a new invented school question written so each holder would agree, and a talk plan) and the check-back rehearsal from an unseen card. Carry it out: "so what I hear is..." before any reply in a seminar. Family activity: one family talk where each person says back the other's point first. Tools: the talk turn card, the feelings scale, the two-loop page. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 (unit 19); CASEL.Relationship Skills.1 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.8 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.1 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.4 (unit 24). | First because the grade 10 step (a second person's view) starts in listening; every later unit this year plans with a partner. The ELA and world history leans are in the same weeks (Cross-subject). Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: a seminar from the ELA course used as the Application case, where the school runs both | SA5: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment. SO8: invented systems only; no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
152:| 8 | 10 | Hard conversations. *How do I hold a hard talk when both of us are upset?* | 10 | g10-rel-ask-offer-help; g10-rel-settle-disagreements; g10-sa-name-feelings; g10-sm-big-feelings; g10-sm-calm-down; g10-sm-have-a-say | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-rl-complex-characters, characters whose motives conflict and change | Two groups. Group 1, the talk itself: an invented hard conversation over a shared commitment planned and held with a partner, each writing the other's interests first, a fix agreed with the I-statement frame and checked later (RS5); a coping plan for two people in an invented tense talk (an agreed pause signal, a tool each could use, how to come back to the talk), rehearsed and judged on whether the pause helped both (SM2); an offer of help to an invented friend who may not want it, how the offer sounds from the friend's side, the support map used to plan when an adult must come in, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS8). Group 2, feelings on both sides and having a say: the feelings of both people in an invented hard conversation charted, where they set each other off, and where naming a feeling aloud could slow the talk (SA3); a plan for keeping a strong feeling from driving an invented hard talk, with how the other person's feeling might rise too, judged in a rehearsed scene (SM1); a plan to raise a class concern with a teacher or school leader (an invented crowded test schedule), how the adult may see it, the plan revised to answer that view, judged after a rehearsal (SM7). Retrieval only: grade 9 units 1, 2 and 4 rows (the coping plan, the support map, the I-statement frame) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page for a new invented hard talk (both sides' interests and feelings, the pause signal, the fix) and the talk rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the agreed pause signal in class discussions. Family activity: agree one pause signal at home. Tools: the coping plan page, the I-statement frame, the support map, the two-person feelings chart. Curio Class writes the cases and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.1 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.2 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 (unit 24); CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (unit 24). | Second because hard talks need the listening of unit 7, and they come before the limits and refusals of unit 10. Six sub-skills, two groups of three. Sessions 11 to 20 of the year, weeks 6 to 10. | Candidate: a rehearsal where the teacher plays an adult who says no, so the student plans the next step | RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps; the friend's trouble stays a school-day one (a grade slump, a quarrel), not a health topic. SM2, SA3: no clinical words; feelings named for characters in checks. RS5: the teacher plays any upset part. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
153:| 9 | 10 | Culture and respect. *How do people who see the world differently work and live well together?* | 9 | g10-rel-different-ways; g10-sa-my-strengths; g10-sa-snap-judgments; g10-sa-what-interests-me; g10-sa-who-i-am; g10-soc-fair-rules; g10-soc-notice-good | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-rl-world-literature, stories from under colonial rule, told from inside | Three groups. Group 1, unfair norms and difference: the fairness questions applied to a settled case, Brown v. Board of Education (1954), where the Supreme Court struck down school segregation, taught as settled fact with dignity; how the norm looked to the people it held down and to those it favored; the unwritten rules of two settings compared and a fair change planned (SO6); working norms planned with a partner from another background in an invented mixed team, each writing how the other sees a clash (eye contact, directness, time), judged after a scene (RS3); how an invented student could check a snap judgment about a classmate from another school, how the judged classmate would experience the guess, judged on whether the plan corrected it (SA6). Group 2, self and newcomer: how an invented character sees their own groups and how a newcomer sees them, a plan for making room while staying themselves (SA1); how an invented team could use a member's strength from a home language or culture (translating for a visitor, a family tradition of storytelling), with how the member might want it used or not (SA2). Group 3, credit and purpose: credit planned for an invented teammate whose strength looks different from the others', judged by how the teammate would hear it (SO2); privately, an interest weighed against what another person expects (a friend, a coach), that person's view written fairly, judged after a set time (SA9). Retrieval only: grade 9 unit 3 and 5 rows in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen page (team norms for a new invented mixed team, with each side's view) and the snap-judgment rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 7 and 8. Carry it out: one question asked before a first judgment of a new classmate, kept private. Family activity: a family story of a custom another family showed them. Tools: the fairness questions page, the team norms page, the six-step stop-think-choose card. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 (unit 19); CASEL.Social Awareness.2 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.6 (unit 22). | Third because the grade 10 view-taking of units 7 and 8 is now turned on difference and fairness; the world history course teaches apartheid in weeks 22 to 25, after this unit, so SEL uses Brown. Seven sub-skills, three groups (3, 2, 2). Sessions 21 to 29 of the year, weeks 11 to 15, then the mid-year check (sessions 30 and 31). | Candidate: a second settled case from the world history course's later unit, read back in the year-end check | SO6: settled facts the courts ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content; the history itself is social studies'. RS3, SA1, SA2: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from invented partners, each shown as one person's way, never a whole culture's. SA6: never blame of a group. SA9: the list stays with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity (the Brown sources first). |
154:| 10 | 10 | Saying no, setting limits. *How do I hold a limit without losing a friend?* | 11 | g10-rds-think-ahead; g10-rel-friendships; g10-rel-say-no; g10-rel-stand-up; g10-sa-tell-truth; g10-soc-read-situation | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-w-narrative, a narrative that turns on a choice | Three groups. Group 1, limits in friendship: a limit set in an invented friendship (a friend who wants every free period together), how the friend may hear it, the talk planned with the I-statement frame and check-back, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS2); a refusal for an invented case where a friend presses the student to lie to a teacher, how the friend sees the ask, an alternative that keeps the friendship, rehearsed against the teacher or a card character with the second push (RS6). Group 2, read it and think ahead: an invented weekend plan that turns uneasy read from the student's side and a friend's, whether to stay, change it or leave, judged by both people's results (SO7); a choice in an invented case (saying yes to every favor a friend asks) with results for self and the friend over a month (RD4). Group 3, truth and standing up: what to say in an invented case where telling the truth lets down a friend, how the friend and the teacher each see it (SA4); how to stand up for an invented classmate mocked online by a school group: first asking the classmate what they want, then the safest option with the I-statement frame, both rehearsed with the teacher, who plays the classmate and the group (RS9). Retrieval only: grade 9 unit 4 and 6 rows and unit 8's coping plan in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen refusal plan page (the friend's view, the alternative, the exit) and the refusal rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the alternative offer ("I can't do that, but I can...") when a friend asks too much. Family activity: the family names one limit and how to say it kindly. Tools: the I-statement frame, the six-step stop-think-choose card, refusal cards. Curio Class writes the cases and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Social Awareness.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.9 (unit 22); CASEL.Relationship Skills.6 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4 (unit 23); CASEL.Self-Awareness.4 (unit 23); CASEL.Relationship Skills.2 (unit 24). | Fourth because limits rest on the hard-talk plan of unit 8 and the respect of unit 9. Health grade 9 unit 10 (limits, consent and conflict) taught the boundary steps a year before; SEL opens with them as retrieval where the school ran health. Six sub-skills, three groups. Sessions 32 to 42 of the year, weeks 16 to 21. | Candidate: an online version of the refusal (a group chat) as the third Application case | RS2, RS6: no real friends named; the teacher or a card plays the pressure; dating, consent in relationships and substances are health's (health units 10, 12, 19 and 20). SO7: the uneasy weekend plan stays a school-day kind of unease (a plan that breaks a rule, a ride with no plan home stops at "call an adult"), never a substance or party case. RS9: no student plays the mocked classmate or the group. SA4: checked on the case only. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
155:| 11 | 10 | Teams that work. *How do we build a team plan that works for every member?* | 11 | g10-rel-lead-follow; g10-rel-teamwork; g10-sm-plan-and-tidy; g10-sm-set-goal; g10-sm-stay-on-task; g10-sm-step-forward; g10-soc-care-when-struggle; g10-soc-thankful | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-w-research, a team research project with handoffs | Three groups. Group 1, the team plan: the team designs its plan, each member writes how the split looks from one other member's side, and the team adjusts and judges it at the project's end (RS4; it also begins AASL.III.D.2); a team timeline where tasks hand off between members, what each handoff asks of the next person, buffers, judged after an invented delay (SM5); a shared goal on the goal steps plan page with what each member needs for it to work, revised after a check-in where needs clash (SM4). Group 2, lead, follow and start: a team session planned and led, feedback asked from a teammate, then following that teammate's lead, both roles judged from the other person's side (RS7); a team improvement started with a quiet member invited to co-own it, how the start looks from that member's side (SM6); a focus plan with a study partner (shared work blocks, a signal for off-task, a check-in), what the partner needs from it (SM3; it also begins AASL.V.D.1). Group 3, care and thanks inside the team: care an invented struggling teammate would welcome (asked about first), weighed against the team's deadline (SO3); thanks for each member at the project's end, how each might want it (in front of the team or in private) (SO5). Retrieval only: grade 9 unit 1 and 6 rows (the goal steps plan page, the team plan page) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen team plan page for a new invented team (handoffs, each member's needs, a buffer) and the leading rehearsal from an unseen card. Carry it out: the team's handoff note in any group project this month. Family activity: plan one shared family job with handoffs. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the team plan page, the timeline strip. Curio Class writes the cases and the project. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Management.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.5 (unit 20); CASEL.Relationship Skills.4 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Self-Management.6 (unit 21); CASEL.Self-Management.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.3 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.5 (unit 24). | Fifth because a team plan pulls on listening (unit 7), hard talks (unit 8) and limits (unit 10). PE grade 10 unit 9 (games across cultures, weeks 11 to 15) judges leadership in teams before this unit. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 43 to 53 of the year, weeks 22 to 27. | Candidate: the ELA research team as the Application project, where the school runs both | SO3: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps. RS4, RS7: the team's own task stays school work. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
156:| 12 | 10 | Fair to everyone. *How do we make a choice that is fair to people who see it differently?* | 9 | g10-rds-ask-questions; g10-rds-careful-thinking; g10-rds-check-facts; g10-rds-good-for-us; g10-rds-solve-problems; g10-rds-who-is-affected; g10-sa-i-can-try; g10-sa-skills-grow | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-ri-argument-evaluate, delineating and evaluating a full argument | Three groups. Group 1, questions and facts from two sides: a partner with the other view interviewed before a class decision on an invented fairness question (who gets first pick of project topics), questions that test both views (RD1); two invented students' claims about the fairness of a school rule checked against a two-way table, why each believed their claim, a decision on what the data show (RD2); the six-step stop-think-choose run with a partner who reaches a different choice, the steps where they split compared (RD5). Group 2, who it touches and what is good for all: who an invented school decision affects, with a partner playing one affected group adding what the map missed and the rules it touches, an option fair to both (RD7); a fair split of an invented household's weekly chores among siblings with different schedules, each sibling's view (RD6); a fairness problem in school life the class can act on (how lab partners are chosen), a fix designed with a partner who sees it differently, piloted where allowed and judged from both views (RD3). Group 3, belief and growth beside another person: a plan for an invented friend who doubts they can try out for a selective program (two steps, a helper, a fallback, what the friend would find encouraging) (SA7); a practice plan from a coach's or teacher's invented feedback, how the feedback giver sees the gap (SA8; it also begins AASL.V.D.2). Retrieval only: grade 9 unit 2 and 5 rows (the six steps, the stakeholder map) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the year-end course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen decision page (a new invented fairness question with a two-way table and two views) and the six-step rehearsal with a partner from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 10 and 11. Carry it out: the fairness pilot, run between sessions. Family activity: a chore split weighed from each person's side. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the stakeholder map page, a printed two-way table. Curio Class writes the cases and the data. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the pilot's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 (unit 20); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 (unit 23). | Last because a fair choice uses every grade 10 step: two views, the data, who is affected; the Geometry two-way table unit (unit 8, weeks 29 to 32) falls in the same weeks, and grade 8 math taught two-way tables, so the page reads one without needing the unit. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 54 to 62 of the year, weeks 27 to 31, then the year-end check (sessions 63 and 64, week 32). | Candidate: the fairness pilot's result read back as the year-end check's sampled case | RD6: the household case is invented; no student's own home is asked about. SA7: the course never records a student's own answer. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the fairness pilot inside school). |
157:| 13 | 11 | What matters to me. *What do I hold on to when holding on costs me something?* | 10 | g11-sa-feel-think-link; g11-sa-my-strengths; g11-sa-tell-truth; g11-sa-what-interests-me; g11-sa-who-i-am | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e11-routine-writing, short reflective writing on stories built on doubt | Two groups. Group 1, values with a price: in an invented case where acting on a value costs a spot (reporting a teammate's rule break before a final game), what to do and say, the price named, judged with the look-back step (SA4); how pressure (a deadline, a parent's hope, a friend's push) runs through an invented student's thoughts and feelings to a choice against what they value, where to break the loop (SA5); privately, how to give time to an interest when something else must give (a paid job, a sport, study hours), the cost of each path, judged after a set time (SA9). Group 2, self and strength under pressure: how an invented eleventh grader keeps what matters when two groups (a team and a job) each pull hard, what holding steady costs (SA1); how an invented character could use a strength where showing it carries a cost (a quiet student leading a debate, a bilingual student always asked to translate for free), the cost weighed (SA2). Retrieval only: grade 10 units 7, 9 and 10 rows in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a new invented case where a value costs something: the choice, the price, the look-back) and the truth-telling talk rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: one written "what it cost, was it worth it" line after a hard choice this month, kept private. Family activity: a family story of a choice that cost something and was worth it. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the loop page, the identity sketch page. Curio Class writes the cases. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.4 (unit 23). | First because the grade 11 step (a cost or a pressure) starts in what the student values; every later plan this year names its price. Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: a second Fluency case where the cost falls on a friend rather than the student | SA1, SA2: family and culture never asked about by name; a student may make up the sketch. SA4: checked on the case only. SA9: the list stays with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
158:| 14 | 11 | Plans and setbacks. *How do I keep going when the plan breaks?* | 11 | g11-sa-i-can-try; g11-sa-name-feelings; g11-sa-skills-grow; g11-sm-big-feelings; g11-sm-calm-down; g11-sm-plan-and-tidy; g11-sm-set-goal; g11-sm-stay-on-task | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e11-research, a research project on reform with a long timeline | Three groups. Group 1, the long plan: a long-term goal plan for an invented eleventh grader (a skill certificate, a team captaincy) on the goal steps plan page, what it costs in time and other wants, revised and judged after an invented setback (SM4); an invented overloaded month (courses, a part-time job, a family duty), what to cut and the cost of each cut, judged on what the plan protected (SM5); a restart plan for an invented student whose focus slumps midway through a long project after a bad grade, judged from a case log (SM3; it also begins AASL.V.D.1). Group 2, try again and grow: a second try for an invented character who failed in public (a driving test, an audition), with two steps, one place to ask and a fallback, the cost of trying again (SA7); a practice plan for an invented student who has hit a plateau with little time before an exam, what to change and what to drop, never belief alone (SA8; it also begins AASL.V.D.2); an invented student's coping plan revised for a high-pressure term (exams, a job, a game season), sleep and rest kept in, what it costs, reviewed after the case term (SM2). Group 3, the feeling after a loss: a response plan for a strong feeling after an invented setback (a rejection letter, a lost final), what reacting would cost, judged in a second scene (SM1); an invented student's feelings charted across a term of rising pressure, exact words as they shift, where to catch early signs (SA3). Retrieval only: grade 10 units 8, 11 and 12 rows (the coping plan, the goal plan page) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a new invented setback: the revised plan, what it cuts, the coping step) and the response plan rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the student's own revised plan after one setback this month, kept private. Family activity: the family's plan for its busiest month. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the coping plan page, the feelings scale, the case log. Curio Class writes the cases and logs. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.1 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.2 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.5 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.4 (unit 24). | Second because a setback plan needs the values of unit 13, and junior year's first heavy stretch falls here. PE grade 10 unit 8 (a fitness plan with rest) taught rest and sleep in a plan a year before; SEL opens with it as retrieval. The ELA research unit comes in weeks 12 to 16, after: SEL's long plan is the planning page the student can carry there. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 11 to 21 of the year, weeks 6 to 11. | Candidate: the plateau practice plan tried on one real skill the student chooses, kept private | SM2, SM1, SA3: no clinical words; SEL is not therapy; feelings named for characters in checks. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health grade 9 units 7 and 21). Disclosure: health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
159:| 15 | 11 | Weighing evidence for choices. *How do I decide well when the choice costs and the clock is running?* | 8 | g11-rds-ask-questions; g11-rds-careful-thinking; g11-rds-check-facts; g11-rds-think-ahead; g11-sa-snap-judgments | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:us1-research-inquiry, the research cycle and its questions | Two groups. Group 1, questions and facts with a price: an inquiry on an invented choice with pressure to decide fast (a summer program with a deadline tomorrow): questions, sources, what would change one's mind, and what the time pressure cost the inquiry (RD1); the claims behind an invented costly choice (two programs with different fees and results) checked against the numbers, what the evidence cannot settle (RD2); the short- and long-term costs of options in an invented choice that is hard to undo (dropping a course for more work hours), judged as the case unfolds (RD4). Group 2, careful under pressure: the six-step stop-think-choose run on an invented choice under pressure from a friend and a deadline, then which step the pressure tried to skip and what skipping it would have cost (RD5); how an invented student could check a snap judgment a whole group shares about a new team member when disagreeing costs standing (SA6). Retrieval only: grade 10 unit 12 rows and the six steps in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen decision page (a new invented choice with two offers, its numbers and a deadline) and the six-step rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 13 and 14. Carry it out: the "what would change my mind" question before one real decision, kept private. Family activity: compare two prices for one family purchase. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the decision page, printed offer sheets. Curio Class writes the cases and offers. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 (unit 23). | Third because a choice under a deadline needs the setback plan of unit 14; the US History I research unit (unit 1, course days 1 to 19) taught the research cycle in the fall. Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 22 to 29 of the year, weeks 11 to 15, then the mid-year check (sessions 30 and 31). | Candidate: an offer sheet with a hidden fee, found only by the planned questions | SA6: invented cases only, never blame of a group. RD2: the offers are invented, no real school or company named. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
160:| 16 | 11 | Working with others. *How do we work well together when the work is hard and the time is short?* | 11 | g11-rel-different-ways; g11-rel-settle-disagreements; g11-rel-talk-listen; g11-rel-teamwork; g11-soc-notice-good; g11-soc-read-situation; g11-soc-their-view | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e11-seminar, initiating and taking part in collaborative discussion | Three groups. Group 1, talk and settle: an invented talk where saying the plain truth may cost goodwill (telling a team its plan will not meet the deadline), planned and rehearsed with the check-back step (RS1); a negotiation on an invented team disagreement where the only workable deal costs one member more than the other (a member who must give up a weekend so the team keeps its date), planned and held with a partner, a fix agreed with the I-statement frame and a follow-up check that tests whether the uneven cost was made up (RS5). Group 2, views and credit at a cost: the view of an invented junior teammate on a mostly senior team who proposes a slower, safer plan, stated well enough that the teammate would agree though it costs the student standing with the seniors, and how to raise it (SO1); specific credit for an invented junior teammate whose idea replaced the student's own (SO2); an invented work-group moment where the stakes rise (a group plan to skip a step that keeps a project safe), whether to stay, change it or leave, each cost named (SO7). Group 3, the team under loss: working norms for an invented team where habits clash and the deadline is close, what each side gives up (RS3); a team plan for a project with a hard deadline, an invented loss (a member drops out), replanned without blame, judged on what the plan protected (RS4; it also begins AASL.III.D.2). Retrieval only: grade 10 units 7, 8, 9 and 11 rows (the talk turn card with check-back, the team plan page) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen team plan page after an invented loss and the hard-truth talk rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the follow-up check on one team agreement this month. Family activity: replan one family task after something falls through. Tools: the talk turn card, the I-statement frame, the team plan page. Curio Class writes the cases and the project. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.1 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.4 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.2 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 (unit 22); CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.1 (unit 24). | Fourth because teamwork under loss draws on the evidence checks of unit 15 and the setback plans of unit 14. PE grade 11 unit 15 (lead a session, weeks 12 to 15) comes before, where students take the elective. Seven sub-skills, three groups (2, 3, 2). Sessions 32 to 42 of the year, weeks 16 to 21. | Candidate: the ELA seminar used as the hard-truth talk's Application, where the school runs both | RS3: no student is asked to explain their own culture. SO7: the skipped step is a school-project safety step (eye protection in a lab build), never a substance or risk-taking case. RS5: the teacher plays any upset part. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
161:| 17 | 11 | Help, support and pressure. *How do I give and get help when it costs, and push back on pressure that costs me?* | 11 | g11-rel-ask-offer-help; g11-rel-friendships; g11-rel-say-no; g11-soc-care-when-struggle; g11-soc-read-feelings; g11-soc-thankful; g11-soc-why-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:us1-news-reading, telling news from opinion | Three groups. Group 1, help with a cost: asking for help when it costs (asking an invented manager at a part-time job to move a shift before finals, knowing it may cost hours) and offering help that takes real time, the support map for each, rehearsed aloud with a partner playing the manager and then the friend (RS8); an invented friendship where a friend asks for daily help with work the student has no time for, a limit planned with the I-statement frame and its check-back, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS2); a refusal plan for an invented case where saying no carries a price (being dropped from a group trip for refusing to cover a lie), rehearsed against the teacher or a card character (RS6). Group 2, care that lasts: care for an invented friend whose hard time goes on for weeks, what the care costs the student and when to bring in an adult (SO3); the feelings of an invented teammate whose replies grow short over a week of messages after being cut from the starting line-up, a check-in that respects the teammate even if the group teases the student for it (SO4). Group 3, thanks and pressure from systems: thanks for an invented person whose help cost them (a friend who gave up an evening to help study) (SO5); how an invented media feed and an invented peer norm press students toward a costly habit (buying the newest gear, staying up for streaks), one change designed to resist it and what it would cost (SO8). Retrieval only: grade 10 units 8, 10 and 11 rows (the support map, the refusal plan) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a support map and a limit for a new invented case) and the refusal rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: one thanks matched to what the help cost, given this month, kept private. Family activity: the family's support map updated for the year. Tools: the support map, the I-statement frame, refusal cards, mock feed pages. Curio Class writes the cases, feeds and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Social Awareness.8 (unit 22); CASEL.Relationship Skills.6 (unit 23); CASEL.Relationship Skills.2 (unit 24); CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.3 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.5 (unit 24). | Fifth because help and pushback under a cost need the team work of unit 16 and the evidence checks of unit 15; the US History I news unit (unit 9, weeks 28 to 31) comes after, so SEL's feed pages need no news-reading skill. Health grade 9 unit 8 (help and concern) taught asking for help on health concerns two years before. Seven sub-skills, three groups (3, 2, 2). Sessions 43 to 53 of the year, weeks 22 to 27. | Candidate: the costly-habit change tried by the class for a week as the carry-out | RS2, RS6: no real friends named; the teacher or a card plays the pressure; dating and substances are health's (health units 12, 19, 20). SO3: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps; suicide and self-harm help is health's (unit 21). SO8: invented feeds only; no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
162:| 18 | 11 | Acting on what matters. *What would I do to make a rule fairer, and what am I willing to give up?* | 9 | g11-rds-good-for-us; g11-rds-solve-problems; g11-rds-who-is-affected; g11-rel-lead-follow; g11-rel-stand-up; g11-sm-have-a-say; g11-sm-step-forward; g11-soc-fair-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:us1-jim-crow, the long-term effects of one Jim Crow practice | Three groups. Group 1, fairness that costs: the fairness questions applied to a settled case, Morgan v. Virginia (1946), where the Supreme Court struck down a state's segregation of interstate buses, taught as settled fact with dignity, with what changing the norm cost the people who did it; beside it an invented school norm whose change would cost the student's own group a privilege (seniors' first pick of parking or lunch tables) (SO6); who an invented senior trip date affects when it suits most seniors but clashes with a large group's work shifts, an option fair to all and the rules it touches (RD7); an invented choice where well-being for self and others costs something wanted (a weekend shift against a promise to a younger sibling) (RD6). Group 2, start and lead at a cost: the start an invented senior could make on a peer tutoring slot that costs a free period, others recruited (SM6); leading an invented group that must cut one member's part to meet a deadline, the hard talk rehearsed with a partner playing the member, then following a leader the student disagrees with (RS7); the class plans a proposal to change one school routine that staff time limits (a later library hour), its costs for staff and groups, answers to objections, a part carried out where the school allows (SM7). Group 3, solve and stand up: a school problem the class can act on with a limited budget of time or money, a fix within the limit, piloted and judged (RD3); an upstander plan for an invented school case where the unkind act comes from a teammate the student depends on for a shared grade, the words rehearsed while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act (RS9). Retrieval only: grade 10 units 9, 11 and 12 rows in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the year-end course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen plan page (a new invented norm change with its cost) and the upstander rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 16 and 17. Carry it out: the routine-change proposal, followed through. Family activity: the family weighs one choice where doing right costs something. Tools: the fairness questions page, the stakeholder map page, the goal steps plan page. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the pilot's supplies, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Self-Management.6 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.9 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Management.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 (unit 23). | Last because acting on a fair change pulls on every grade 11 step (a value, a cost, the evidence, the team); the US History I Jim Crow unit (unit 6, weeks 20 to 22) comes before, so the court case lands on known history. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 54 to 62 of the year, weeks 27 to 31, then the year-end check (sessions 63 and 64, week 32). | Candidate: the pilot's budget sheet read back in the year-end check | SO6: settled facts the courts ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content; the history is social studies'. SM7: no cause picked by the course. RS9: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly. Agent reads: sensitivity (the Morgan sources first); safety (the pilot inside school). |
163:| 19 | 12 | Looking back, looking ahead. *Who am I taking with me past school, and what will I try first?* | 10 | g12-sa-feel-think-link; g12-sa-i-can-try; g12-sa-my-strengths; g12-sa-what-interests-me; g12-sa-who-i-am | CASEL.Self-Awareness.1; CASEL.Self-Awareness.2; CASEL.Self-Awareness.5; CASEL.Self-Awareness.7; CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e12-narrative, reflective writing that tells one's own story | Owner year for every code. Two groups. Group 1, who I am and what I bring: a looking-ahead sketch for an invented senior (or one the student makes up) moving into work, training or college, bringing together the personal parts and the groups the person belongs to (family, culture, interests, roles), which to carry, which groups to leave and join, the trade-offs, judged after a scene set in a first job (SA1); strengths named for an invented senior's job interview or application, one from a home language or culture among them, each matched to what the setting asks (SA2); how an invented senior's thoughts about leaving school (a missed application read as "I will never get in") and what the senior cares about (staying near family, earning early) shape feelings and choices, where to break the loop, judged in a scene after graduation (SA5). Group 2, purpose and a first step: privately, a next step after school that fits an interest (a course, a trade, a job, a year of service) and gives effort a purpose, its trade-offs, a date to judge whether it still fits (SA9); an unfamiliar adult task for an invented senior (applying for a first apartment, starting a training program), two steps, a place to ask and a fallback, the belief that it can be done turned into action (SA7). Retrieval only: grade 11 unit 13 and 14 rows in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a looking-ahead sketch and a first-step plan for a new invented senior) and the interview strengths rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the student's own next-step date, kept private. Family activity: the family's story of a first step after school. Tools: the identity sketch page, the asset plan page, the goal steps plan page. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. | First because the owner year looks past school from the start; the sketch and the next step frame every later grade 12 plan. The ELA lean is in the same weeks. Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: the interview rehearsal with an invited school adult playing the interviewer | SA1, SA2: family and culture never asked about by name; home languages welcome when offered; a student may make up the sketch. SA7, SA9: the course never records a student's own answer; the list stays with the student. SA5: not a treatment. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
164:| 20 | 12 | Steady under change. *How do I stay steady when school's routines are gone?* | 11 | g12-sa-name-feelings; g12-sa-skills-grow; g12-sm-big-feelings; g12-sm-calm-down; g12-sm-plan-and-tidy; g12-sm-stay-on-task | CASEL.Self-Awareness.3; CASEL.Self-Awareness.8; CASEL.Self-Management.1; CASEL.Self-Management.2; CASEL.Self-Management.3; CASEL.Self-Management.5 | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e12-routine-writing, short daily writing that keeps a routine | Owner year for every code. Three groups. Group 1, notice and handle the feeling: an invented graduate's feelings charted through a first month at work or college, mixed feelings named exactly, early signs to watch (SA3); a response plan for an invented strong-feeling moment at a first job (a sharp correction from a supervisor in front of others), so the feeling guides the response rather than drives it: the early sign, a calm-down step, a response and a repair, judged in a rehearsed scene (SM1). Group 2, cope and keep going: a coping plan for an invented graduate's first term away from school routines (a new schedule, a job, living with others), ways to calm down and cope for each setting, rest kept in, a back-up, a review date, and where to find help after school named in plain words (SM2); a focus plan for an invented graduate with no one checking daily (an online course, a job with loose hours), self check-ins, pushing oneself without being made to (SM3; it also teaches AASL.V.D.1, trying again when a task gets hard, placed in SEL by library, owned here). Group 3, keep order and grow: a planning system for an invented graduate's month (work shifts, classes, bill dates, appointments) with its steps and time plan, and the papers and materials kept in order (pay stubs, a lease, a class folder), judged after an invented change of shift (SM5); a practice plan for a new skill an invented graduate must learn at work or in training, with a supervisor's feedback, effort, practice and help, never belief alone (SA8; it also teaches AASL.V.D.2, skill grows with practice and help, owned here). Retrieval only: grade 11 unit 14 rows and the coping plan in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen coping plan page for a new invented graduate and the correction response rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the student's own coping plan, reviewed once, kept private. Family activity: a plan for one family change (a move, a new job). Tools: the feelings scale, the coping plan page, the goal steps plan page, a sample month. Curio Class writes the cases and logs. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. | Second because a plan for life past school needs the steadying tools first; PE grade 12 unit 21 (steady in hard moments, weeks 12 to 15) comes after and opens with the coping plan. Six sub-skills, three groups of two. Sessions 11 to 21 of the year, weeks 6 to 11. | Candidate: a second correction scene where the supervisor is partly wrong, so the repair is a calm reply rather than an apology | No clinical words; SEL is not therapy. Breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help, health grade 9 units 7 and 21), named in the teacher notes. Disclosure: health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
165:| 21 | 12 | Leading well. *How do I lead, and follow, with adults outside school?* | 8 | g12-rel-lead-follow; g12-rel-talk-listen; g12-rel-teamwork; g12-sm-step-forward; g12-soc-notice-good; g12-soc-read-situation | CASEL.Relationship Skills.1; CASEL.Relationship Skills.4; CASEL.Relationship Skills.7; CASEL.Self-Management.6; CASEL.Social Awareness.2; CASEL.Social Awareness.7 | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e12-seminar, collaborative discussion on grade 12 texts and issues | Owner year for every code. Two groups. Group 1, lead the team: an invented community event team (a food drive, a youth game day) led well, tasks delegated, then following another leader in a second role and knowing when to follow, both judged from team feedback (RS7); a charter for a project that reaches past school (a service project with a community partner): roles, norms, how the team solves problems together and raises them, carried out and judged in a closing review (RS4; it also teaches AASL.III.D.2, the group's learning as one's own job, owned here); a start for something a community group or workplace needs (a sign-up sheet, a welcome for new volunteers), stepping forward without being asked, first steps, recruits, costs (SM6). Group 2, talk and read the setting: a talk in a setting past school (asking a supervisor to change a schedule, a call with a community partner), the channel and words chosen, speaking and listening with the check-back step so meaning gets across (RS1); specific credit a leader in an invented workplace or community group gives each member over a project (SO2); invented settings past school (a first shift, a volunteer meeting, a job interview) read for what each asks and the chances it offers, how to act in each, judged against the case (SO7). Retrieval only: grade 11 unit 16 and 18 rows (the team plan page, the talk turn card) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks)