76:measurement-data,3,m3-scaled-graphs,3.MD.B.3,m2-picture-bar-graphs,m6-data-displays,bar-graph,bar-graph,scale; key; picture graph; bar graph; category; how many more; how many fewer,science:3-ESS2-1 (weather data shown in tables and bar graphs uses the scaled graph read here),"Draw and read scaled picture graphs (one picture stands for 2, 5 or 10 things) and scaled bar graphs, with six or fewer categories, and solve one- and two-step how many more and how many fewer problems from them. The scale makes each reading a small multiplication, which is why IM grade 3 unit 1 opens multiplication with these graphs. Limits: whole numbers, products within 100 for the scale reading. Cluster 3.MD.B is supporting: it practises the major multiplication work. Progressions MD PDF 85 (79). Builds on grade 2 picture and bar graphs with a scale of 1 (2.MD.D.10, from the K to 2 band). No grade 4 or 5 standard extends categorical graphs; the next data graph is grade 6 (histograms, 6.SP.B.4), so this row hands on across the band edge only (see the edges file)."
155:ratios-proportions,6,m6-equivalent-ratios,6.RP.A.3a,m5-graph-points; m5-two-patterns; m5-multiply-algorithm,m7-scale-drawings; m7-proportional-tables; m7-proportional-graphs; m7-circles,number-line; coordinate-plane; tape-diagram,double-number-line; ratio-table,equivalent ratios; double number line; table; row; column; same rate; compare,,"IM 6.2, lessons 6 to 16; cluster 6.RP.A, major. Students make equivalent ratios by multiplying both quantities by the same number, record them on double number lines and in ratio tables, find missing values, plot the pairs on the first-quadrant coordinate plane (they fall on a line through the origin) and use tables to compare two ratios (same shade of paint, same speed). Introduces the double-number-line (best when the units differ) and the ratio-table. The reasoning grows from repeated addition to scaling by multiplication and division (Progressions RP 6-7, PDF pages 166 to 167 (printed 160 to 161)). Limit: whole-number measurements in the tables; no proportion equations (cross multiplying is not required in grade 6, printed page 161)."
156:ratios-proportions,6,m6-ratio-parts-problems,6.RP.A.3,m5-fraction-mult-problems,m7-percent-problems,tape-diagram; ratio-table; double-number-line,tape-diagram; ratio-table; double-number-line,part; whole; total; tape diagram; strategy,,"IM 6.2, lessons 14 to 17 (part-part-whole and ""solving more ratio problems""); cluster 6.RP.A, major. Owns the parent standard: students choose among tables, tape diagrams, double number lines and equations to solve ratio problems, including part-part-whole (""a 3 to 2 mix, 85 cups in all"") with a tape diagram, and say why their choice fits. Lettered parts are owned by m6-equivalent-ratios (a), m6-unit-rate (b), m6-percent (c) and m6-unit-conversion (d). Numbers: whole numbers and simple fractions of a part. Progressions RP 6-7, PDF page 168 (printed 162)."
157:ratios-proportions,6,m6-unit-rate,6.RP.A.2; 6.RP.A.3b,m5-fraction-as-division; m5-divide-two-digit,m7-proportional-tables; m7-unit-rate-fractions,ratio-table; double-number-line,ratio-table; double-number-line,rate; unit rate; per; each; unit price; constant speed; best buy,science:MS-ESS2-2 (rates such as millimeters per year),"IM 6.3 (Unit Rates and Percentages), lessons 5 to 9, and IM 6.2 speed lessons; cluster 6.RP.A, major. For a ratio a : b with b not 0, students find the two unit rates (a/b of the first per 1 of the second, and b/a the other way), use ""per"" and ""for each 1"", and solve unit price and constant speed problems (""7 hours to mow 4 lawns, how many lawns in 35 hours""). Unit rates are especially useful for finding entries (printed page 162). Limit: unit rates with non-complex fractions only (the standards footnote, Progressions RP 6-7, PDF page 166 (printed 160)); rates of fractions per fractions wait for grade 7."
158:ratios-proportions,6,m6-unit-conversion,6.RP.A.3d,m5-measurement-conversion,m7-unit-rate-fractions,double-number-line; ratio-table,double-number-line; ratio-table,convert; unit; inch; foot; centimeter; meter; liter; kilogram; conversion rate,,"IM 6.3, lessons 1 to 4; cluster 6.RP.A, major. Students treat ""12 inches in a foot"" or ""1,000 grams in a kilogram"" as a rate and convert with a double number line, a table or a unit rate, deciding whether the number should get bigger or smaller (smaller unit, more of them). Customary and metric units; whole-number and decimal measurements. Limit: one conversion at a time (no derived units such as square feet to square yards). Progressions RP 6-7, PDF page 168 (printed 162). Track units when quantities are multiplied or divided: 12 miles divided by 3 hours gives 4 miles per hour, and 4 miles per hour times 2 hours gives 8 miles. Students explain the resulting unit and why it fits the quantity (the rest of 6.RP.A.3d)."
159:ratios-proportions,6,m6-percent,6.RP.A.3c,m5-read-compare-thousandths; m5-fraction-times-whole,m7-percent-problems; m7-rational-problems; m7-chance-probability; m7-probability-models; m7-compound-events,double-number-line; tape-diagram; number-line,double-number-line; tape-diagram,percent; per hundred; of; whole; part; 100%,,"IM 6.3, lessons 10 to 16; cluster 6.RP.A, major. A percent is a rate per 100: students find a percent of a quantity (30% of 60), find the whole given a part and the percent (""75% of the budget is $1200""), and find what percent one quantity is of another, on double number lines marked 0% to 100%, in tables and with tape diagrams; benchmark percents (10%, 25%, 50%, 75%) first. Limit: no percent increase or decrease, tax, tip or interest (grade 7). Progressions RP 6-7, PDF pages 164 and 168 (printed 158 and 162)."
175:statistics-probability,6,m6-data-displays,6.SP.B.4,m3-scaled-graphs; m5-line-plots,m7-compare-populations; alg1-data-displays,dot-plot; number-line,dot-plot; histogram; box-plot; number-line,dot plot; histogram; bin; frequency; box plot; quartile; five-number summary,,"IM 6.8, lessons 6 to 8 and 15 to 16; cluster 6.SP.B, additional. Students display numerical data on a number line as dot plots, histograms (equal-width bins, bars touching, frequency on the vertical axis) and box plots (minimum, quartiles, median, maximum), and choose the display that suits the data set (histograms for large data sets). Introduces the histogram and box-plot. Limit: numerical data on one variable. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 193 (printed 187). Assumes m3-scaled-graphs from grade 3 across grades 4 and 5 on purpose: neither grade has a categorical graph standard, and reading a scaled axis is what the frequency axis of a histogram uses. Re-entry (revisited): rebuild m3-scaled-graphs briefly before using it, opening with a readiness check; fluent recall after 2 grades without the skill is not assumed."
176:statistics-probability,6,m6-center-variability,6.SP.A.3; 6.SP.B.5c,m5-line-plots; m5-decimal-operations,m7-compare-populations,dot-plot; box-plot; number-line,dot-plot; box-plot,mean; median; mean absolute deviation; interquartile range; range; measure of center; measure of variability,science:MS-PS3-3 (summarize repeated trial data with a measure of center),"IM 6.8, lessons 9 to 16; cluster 6.SP.A and 6.SP.B, additional. A measure of center sums up a data set in one number and a measure of variability says how the values vary in one number. Students find the mean (as fair share and as balance point), the median, the mean absolute deviation and the interquartile range, and describe patterns and striking departures (outliers). Uses the grade 6 decimal fluency of m6-decimal-operations for the arithmetic. Limit: data sets small enough to compute by hand. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF pages 193 to 194 (printed 187 to 188)."
177:statistics-probability,6,m6-summarize-data,6.SP.B.5; 6.SP.B.5a; 6.SP.B.5b; 6.SP.B.5d,m5-line-plots,m7-random-samples,dot-plot; histogram; box-plot,dot-plot; box-plot,observation; attribute; unit of measure; symmetric; skewed; outlier; summary,,"IM 6.8, lessons 12 to 18, the full written summary in lessons 17 and 18 (IM 6.9, an optional unit, is left out and is not needed for the standard); cluster 6.SP.B, additional. Students write a full summary of a numerical data set in context: how many observations, what was measured and how and in what units, the center and variability, and why median and IQR suit a skewed set or one with outliers while mean and MAD suit a roughly symmetric one. Ends with a short data project. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 194 (printed 188)."
184:ratios-proportions,7,m7-percent-problems,7.RP.A.3,m6-percent; m6-ratio-parts-problems,alg1-percent-change-interest,double-number-line; tape-diagram; ratio-table,double-number-line; tape-diagram,percent increase; percent decrease; tax; tip; discount; markup; interest; commission; percent error,science:MS-ESS3-4 (percent increase in population and use); social-studies:ss7-roman-economy (percent increase and tax rates),"IM 7.4, lessons 6 to 16, drawing on the representation work of lessons 4 and 5; cluster 7.RP.A, major. Students solve multistep ratio and percent problems: simple interest, sales tax, markups and markdowns, tips, commission, fees, percent increase and decrease and percent error, seeing ""a 5% increase"" as multiplying by 1.05 (the expression link is owned by m7-linear-expressions). Double number lines and tape diagrams carry the reasoning before equations. Limit: simple interest only, no compound growth (high school). Progressions RP 6-7, PDF page 171 (printed 165). Teacher note: money contexts stay neutral; no student shares family income or debt."
